|uefa cup=0 (Best: [[2003 UEFA Cup final|Runners-up]])|europa conference league=0 (Best: [[2021–22 UEFA Europa Conference League|Knockout Playoff round]])|cup winners' cup=0 (Best: Semi-final)}}
[[Celtic F.C.|Celtic Football Club]] is a [[Scotland|Scottish]] football club based in [[Glasgow]], which has regularly taken part in European competitions since its first appearance in the 1962–63 [[Inter-Cities Fairs Cup]]. Since then, the club has competed in every UEFA-organized competition, with the exception of the [[UEFA Super Cup]] and the defunct [[UEFA Intertoto Cup|Intertoto Cup]].

The club became the first British and Scottish team to win the [[UEFA Champions League|European Cup]] by defeating [[Inter Milan]] in the [[1967 European Cup Final|1967 final]].

Celtic reached a second [[1970 European Cup Final|final]] and lost 2–1 to [[Feyenoord]].

In more recent years, Celtic reached the [[2003 UEFA Cup Final]] in [[Seville]] on 21 May 2003, which they lost 3–2 [[after extra time]] to [[FC Porto|Porto]].

[[Scott Brown (footballer, born June 1985)|Scott Brown]], a long-serving captain for Celtic, holds the club record for the most European appearances, playing in 127 matches. [[Henrik Larsson]], who scored twice in the 2003 UEFA Cup Final, is the record goal scorer of the club, with 35 goals.

==Background==
The first officially sanctioned European club tournament, the [[UEFA Champions League|European Champion Clubs' Cup]], was launched in 1955. Conceived by [[Gabriel Hanot]], the editor of ''[[L'Équipe]]'', as a competition for winners of the European national football leagues, it is considered the most prestigious European football competition.<ref>{{cite book |last=Moore|first=Glenn|title=The Concise Encyclopedia of World Football|publisher=Parragon|isbn=0-7525-4466-7|date=2000|page=217}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/history/ |title=Football's premier club competition |publisher=Union of European Football Associations (UEFA) |access-date=25 December 2011}}</ref> [[Hibernian F.C.|Hibernian]] were amongst the 16 sides invited to take part in the tournament's first season, and they became the first British club to participate in European club competition.<ref>{{cite web|title=Season 1955–56|url=http://www.europeancuphistory.com/euro56.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070713074544/http://www.europeancuphistory.com/euro56.html|url-status=usurped|archive-date=13 July 2007|website=European Cup History|access-date=21 July 2015}}</ref> [[Rangers F.C.|Rangers]] took part in the tournament the following season as champions of Scotland. The [[Inter-Cities Fairs Cup]] was also established at the same time, and in 1960 Hibernian became the first Scottish club to participate, eliminating [[FC Barcelona|Barcelona]] on route to the semi-final, where they lost to [[A.S. Roma|Roma]].<ref>{{cite web|title=Farewell to the Fairs Cup|url=https://splstats.wordpress.com/2012/08/24/farewell-to-the-fairs-cup/|website=SPL Stats|date=24 August 2012 |access-date=10 August 2015}}</ref> The 1960–61 season was also the inaugural year of the [[UEFA Cup Winners' Cup|European Cup Winners' Cup]], with Rangers reaching the final. They were comfortably beaten over two legs by [[ACF Fiorentina|Fiorentina]], but were still the first British club to reach a [[UEFA competitions|UEFA sanctioned]] European club final.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/teams/r/rangers/7359891.stm|title=When Rangers met Fiorentina in '61|last=Murray|first=Keir|date=22 April 2008|website=[[BBC Sport]]|access-date=18 September 2012}}</ref>

==History==

===1960s and 1970s===

====First entries into European competition====
Celtic's third-place finish in the league the previous season saw them qualify for the [[Inter-Cities Fairs Cup]] in 1962. Drawn against Spanish side [[Valencia CF|Valencia]] in the first round, Celtic were defeated 4–2 in the first [[Two-legged tie|leg]] in Spain on 24 October 1962,<ref name="jimc">{{cite web|last=Craig |first=Jim |url=http://jimcraigcsc.wordpress.com/2011/10/23/24th-october-1962-celtic-2-valencia-2-fairs-cup/ |title=24th October 1962 Celtic 2 Valencia 2 Fairs Cup &#124; Jim Craig Celtic Supporter's Club |website=Jimcraigcsc.wordpress.com |date=23 October 2011 |access-date=5 August 2013}}</ref> having trailed 3–0 at half time. [[Bobby Carroll]] scored Celtic's two goals in the second half, becoming the club's first-ever European goal scorer.<ref name="cie">{{cite web|title=Celtic FC in Europe 2012/13|url=https://splstats.wordpress.com/2012/07/18/celtic-fc-in-europe-201213/|website=SPL Stats|access-date=8 June 2015|date=18 July 2012}}</ref> A modicum of pride after the first leg defeat was restored, however, in the return leg in [[Glasgow]], which finished in a 2–2 draw, albeit seeing Celtic lose the tie on aggregate and be eliminated from the competition.<ref name="jimc" /><ref>{{harvnb|Wilson|1988|p=119}}</ref>

The following season, 1963–64, saw Celtic return to European competition, this time in the [[UEFA Cup Winners' Cup|European Cup Winners Cup]]. Celtic belied their mediocre domestic form in Scotland by reaching the semi-final of the tournament, eliminating [[FC Basel|Basel]], [[GNK Dinamo Zagreb|Dinamo Zagreb]] and [[ŠK Slovan Bratislava|Slovan Bratislava]] en route.<ref name="wils122">{{harvnb|Wilson|1988|p=122}}</ref> In Celtic's 5–1 win away against Basel in the first leg of their preliminary round tie on 17 September 1963, [[John Hughes (footballer, born 1943)|John Hughes]] became the first Scottish player to score a [[Hat-trick#Association football|hat-trick]] in the away leg of a European tie.<ref>{{cite web|title=SFAQs|url=http://www.scottishleague.net/archive/archive51.htm|website=www.scottishleague.net|access-date=10 August 2015}}</ref> The first leg of the semi-final against [[MTK Budapest FC|MTK Budapest]] took place at [[Celtic Park]], and goals from [[Jimmy Johnstone]] and [[Stevie Chalmers]] gave Celtic an impressive 3–0 win.<ref name="wils122" /> A combination of tactical naïveté and questionable refereeing saw Celtic slump to a 4–0 defeat in the return leg in Hungary and go out on aggregate.<ref name="wils122" /><ref>{{cite web|title=Celtic fail to reach final|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=wndAAAAAIBAJ&pg=1953%2C4530642|newspaper=The Glasgow Herald|access-date=5 February 2015|page=10|date=30 April 1964}}</ref>

Season 1964–65 saw Celtic back in the Inter-Cities Fairs cup. First-round opponents were Portuguese side [[Leixões S.C.]] The first leg away in Portugal was ill-tempered and marred by eccentric referring decisions in a 1–1 draw. [[Stevie Chalmers]] and [[Ian Young (footballer)|Ian Young]] were both controversially sent off; Chalmers for a minor collision with the Leixões goalkeeper as they both jumped for the ball, Young for a minor scuffle with an opponent (who was also sent off).<ref>{{cite news|last=Henderson|first=Gair|title=Celtic should make a real protest|url=http://image.wikifoundry.com/image/1/D16ip315MjVsU82Nz2Kuww1043183/GW713H1300|access-date=8 December 2016|work=Evening Times|date=24 September 1964|page=22}}</ref> Celtic won the return match in Glasgow 3–0 to progress, although the game was again bad-tempered.<ref>{{cite news|title=Celtic's victory in towsy match|url=http://image.wikifoundry.com/image/1/zIKmI1ONCh0bClm9a3CJow609600/GW450H1092|access-date=8 December 2016|work=The Times|date=8 October 1964|archive-date=20 December 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161220094446/http://image.wikifoundry.com/image/1/zIKmI1ONCh0bClm9a3CJow609600/GW450H1092|url-status=dead}}</ref> In the next round Celtic faced [[FC Barcelona|Barcelona]]. The Spaniards won 3–1 at the [[Nou Camp]] and held Celtic to a goalless draw at Parkhead in a series of ties where they clearly outclassed their opponents.<ref>{{cite news|last=Jacobs|first=Raymond|title=Celtic out of the Fairs Cities Cup - Barcelona too clever|url=http://image.wikifoundry.com/image/1/zqupQuqZhdQSrf-VU-GGsA583919/GW495H1090|access-date=8 December 2016|work=The Times|date=3 December 1964|archive-date=20 December 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161220093448/http://image.wikifoundry.com/image/1/zqupQuqZhdQSrf-VU-GGsA583919/GW495H1090|url-status=dead}}</ref>

====Glory years====
Now with [[Jock Stein]] as manager, Celtic enjoyed another successful run in Europe in 1965–66, reaching the semi-finals of the European Cup Winners' Cup by knocking out [[Go Ahead Eagles|Go Ahead Deventer]], [[Aarhus Gymnastikforening|AGF Aarhus]] and [[FC Dynamo Kyiv|Dynamo Kyiv]].<ref name="wils131">{{harvnb|Wilson|1988|p=131}}</ref><ref name="cw6566">{{cite web |url=http://www.sharkscores.com/Team/Default.aspx?id=65&season=1965%2f1966 |title=Celtic - 1965/1966 |website=SharkScores.com |access-date=5 August 2013 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131109133154/http://www.sharkscores.com/Team/Default.aspx?id=65&season=1965%2F1966 |archive-date=9 November 2013}}</ref> They lost 1–2 on aggregate to [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]] in the semi-final, although a last minute [[Bobby Lennox]] goal was controversially disallowed in the second leg at [[Anfield]] which would have seen Celtic win the tie via the recently implemented [[away goals rule]].<ref name="cw6566" /><ref>{{harvnb|Wilson|1988|p=132}}</ref>

Celtic's [[UEFA Champions League|European Cup]] campaign in [[1966–67 European Cup|1966–67]] was their first-ever participation in Europe's premier club tournament. [[FC Zürich|Zürich]] and [[FC Nantes|Nantes]] were comfortably disposed of in the first two rounds (5–0 and 6–2 on aggregate respectively).<ref>{{cite news|title=Celtic progress in European Cup with utmost ease|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=U-E9AAAAIBAJ&pg=1983%2C828512|access-date=20 March 2017|work=The Glasgow Herald|date=6 October 1966|page=6}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last=Edwards|first=Glyn|title=Uninspired Celtic qualify for quarter-finals|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=P2xAAAAAIBAJ&pg=1713%2C1355769|access-date=20 March 2017|work=The Glasgow Herald|page=6|date=8 December 1966}}</ref> The quarter-final in March 1967 pitched Celtic against [[Yugoslavia|Yugoslav]] champions [[FK Vojvodina|Vojvodina]]. The Yugoslav side won the first leg in [[Novi Sad]] 1–0, while the return match in Glasgow proved to be a fraught affair. The Yugoslavs defended resolutely and threatened on the counter-attack, but Celtic levelled the tie on aggregate in the second half with a goal by Stevie Chalmers.<ref>{{harvnb|Campbell|Woods|1987|p=235}}</ref> Celtic pressed for a winner, but Vojvodina defended well and the tie looked like a play-off in neutral [[Rotterdam]] would be required. In the final minute, however, [[Billy McNeill]] headed in a [[Charlie Gallagher (association footballer)|Charlie Gallacher]] cross to see Celtic progress to the semi-final. Celtic now faced [[Czechoslovakia|Czechoslovak]] side, [[Dukla Prague]]. This time the first leg of the tie took place in Glasgow, with Celtic winning 3–1 courtesy of goals from Jimmy Johnstone and a [[Willie Wallace]] brace.<ref>{{harvnb|Wilson|1988|p=136}}</ref> In respect of his opponents' quality, Stein set up Celtic to be ultra-defensive for the second leg and forsake – temporarily – their philosophy of attacking football.<ref>{{harvnb|Wilson|1988|pp=136–137}}</ref> The tactics worked as Celtic secured a 0–0 draw to put them in the final. Stein, however, was almost apologetic about the manner of Celtic's success in that game and he felt uncomfortable in later years discussing the matter.<ref>{{harvnb|Wilson|1988|p=137}}</ref><ref>{{harvnb|Campbell|Woods|1987|p=236}}</ref>

The final saw Celtic play [[Inter Milan]], with the match taking place at the [[Estádio Nacional]] on the outskirts of [[Lisbon]] on 25 May 1967. Celtic fell a goal behind after only seven minutes, [[Jim Craig (Scottish footballer)|Jim Craig]] adjudged to have fouled [[Renato Cappellini]] in the penalty box and [[Sandro Mazzola]] converting the resultant penalty.<ref name="67match">{{cite news|last=Barham|first=Albert|title=Relentless attack captures European Cup|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/1967/may/25/championsleague.sport|access-date=27 June 2014|newspaper=The Guardian|date=26 May 1967}}</ref> Celtic swept into constant attack after that, but found Inter goalkeeper [[Giuliano Sarti]] in outstanding form.<ref name="67match" /> With 63 minutes played, after incessant pressure Celtic finally equalized when [[Tommy Gemmell]] scored with a powerful 25-yard shot.<ref name="67match" /> The balance of play remained constant, with Inter defending deeply against sustained Celtic attacking. With about five minutes remaining, a long-range shot from [[Bobby Murdoch]] was diverted by Stevie Chalmers past a wrong-footed Sarti.<ref>{{cite web|last=Forsyth|first=Roddy|title=Murdoch the true Lionheart|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/competitions/scottish-premier/3005452/Murdoch-the-true-Lionheart.html|work=The Telegraph|access-date=27 June 2014|date=15 May 2001}}</ref> It proved to be the winning goal and thus Celtic became the first British team, and the first from outside Spain, Portugal or Italy, to win the competition.<ref name="67match" /> The cup-winning side have since become known as the [[Lisbon Lions]].<ref name="guard16">{{cite news|last=Foster|first=Richard|title=Never mind England in 1966, here's Scotland conquering Europe in 1967|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/the-agony-and-the-ecstasy/2016/feb/29/scotland-1967-celtic-rangers-dundee-united-european-cup|access-date=18 March 2017|work=The Guardian|date=29 February 2016}}</ref> Celtic remain the only Scottish club to have reached the final.<ref name="guard16" />

As champions of Europe, Celtic competed in the [[Intercontinental Cup (1960–2004)|Intercontinental Cup]] against South American champions [[Racing Club de Avellaneda|Racing Club]] of Argentina. Both sides won their respective home games and the series went to a deciding game played in [[Montevideo]], Uruguay.{{sfn|Wilson|1988|pp=141–142}} The first two matches, however, were marred by the Argentinians' incessant foul play and spitting,{{sfn|Wilson|1988|pp=141–142}}<ref>{{cite news|last=Henderson|first=Gair|title=Magnificent Celts|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=feRAAAAAIBAJ&pg=4069%2C2661002|access-date=11 May 2015|work=Evening Times|date=19 October 1967|page=26}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=a39AAAAAIBAJ&pg=2051%2C3170400 |newspaper=The Glasgow Herald |date=19 October 1967 |page=6 |title=Victorious Celtic face harder task in Buenos Aires |access-date=29 January 2015 |last=Edwards |first=Glyn}}</ref> and Celtic goalkeeper [[Ronnie Simpson]] was struck on the head by an object thrown by Racing Club fans in the second match in [[Buenos Aires]].<ref>{{cite news|title=Celtic beaten but earn play off|url=http://image.wikifoundry.com/image/1/Ndvve60kvQmkKgWnLqQZYQ483000|access-date=11 May 2015|work=The Times (London)|date=2 November 1967|archive-date=11 December 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221211173213/http://image.wikifoundry.com/image/1/Ndvve60kvQmkKgWnLqQZYQ483000|url-status=dead}}</ref> The third match degenerated into a shambles as the Celtic players lost their composure and discipline. Six players were sent off – four from Celtic and two from Racing Club – with Racing scoring the only goal to win the cup in a match described by ''[[Reuters]]'' as a "bar room brawl".{{sfn|Wilson|1988|pp=141–142}}<ref name="rcba">{{cite news |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=eH5AAAAAIBAJ&pg=2223%2C905818 |newspaper=The Glasgow Herald |date=6 November 1967 |page=6 |title=Referee and both sides blamed for "war" |access-date=29 January 2015}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=Racing 1 – Celtic 0|url=http://www.hailhailmedia.com/racing-1-celtic-0/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150509145108/http://www.hailhailmedia.com/racing-1-celtic-0/|url-status=usurped|archive-date=9 May 2015|website=Hail Hail Media|access-date=12 May 2015}}</ref>

The following two seasons in the European Cup were a disappointment for Celtic; going out to Dynamo Kyiv in the first round in their defence of the trophy in the autumn of 1967,<ref>{{cite web|title=Season 1967–68|url=http://www.europeancuphistory.com/euro68.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070926230629/http://www.europeancuphistory.com/euro68.html|url-status=usurped|archive-date=26 September 2007|website=European Cup History|access-date=21 July 2015}}</ref> then reaching the quarter-finals the following season only to lose their home leg against [[A.C. Milan|AC Milan]], having achieved a credible no-scoring draw in the first game in Italy.<ref>{{cite web|title=Season 1968–69|url=http://www.europeancuphistory.com/euro69.html|archive-url=https://archive.today/20120731025529/http://www.europeancuphistory.com/euro69.html|url-status=usurped|archive-date=31 July 2012|website=European Cup History|access-date=21 July 2015}}</ref> Earlier in the season, Celtic set off a controversy in the wake of the [[Warsaw Pact invasion of Czechoslovakia|Soviet Union's invasion of Czechoslovakia]] in August 1968. At chairman [[Robert Kelly (football chairman)|Robert Kelly]]'s behest, the club sent [[UEFA]] a telegram condemning the military action and expressing concern that any team from Western Europe having to play a tie in an [[Eastern Bloc]] country.<ref name="czech">{{harvnb|Campbell|Woods|1987|pp=406–407}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last=Henderson|first=Gair|title=Salute Celts for courage|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=_xk-AAAAIBAJ&pg=6072%2C2806400|access-date=29 February 2016|work=Evening Times|date=24 August 1968}}</ref> Celtic's stance received support from several clubs throughout Western Europe, although others such as [[Leeds United F.C.|Leeds United]] publicly criticized Celtic's intervention.<ref>{{cite web|title=Political Football no. 2 - Celtic and the Iron Curtain|url=https://theshamrockglasgow.wordpress.com/tag/czechoslovakia-1968/|website=The Shamrock|date=27 June 2014 |access-date=29 February 2016}}</ref> In response, UEFA redid the draw for the first round of the European Cup and European Cup Winners' Cup to avoid pairing Eastern European sides with clubs from the West. This elicited a furious response from clubs and football authorities in the East, the majority of whom withdrew in protest.<ref name="czech" />

Celtic reached the European Cup final again in [[1969–70 European Cup|1970]]. Celtic knocked out Basel in the first round, then defeated Portuguese side [[S.L. Benfica|Benfica]] 3–0 at Parkhead in the first leg of the second round. Celtic's performance was impressive,<ref name="ec6970">{{cite web|title=Season 1969–70|url=http://www.europeancuphistory.com/euro70.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070929083252/http://www.europeancuphistory.com/euro70.html|url-status=usurped|archive-date=29 September 2007|website=European Cup History|access-date=6 October 2013}}</ref> and they were unfortunate not to score more.<ref name="ec6970" /> Benfica rallied in the return match and put Celtic under intense pressure from the start. [[John Fallon (footballer)|John Fallon]] was outstanding in goal for Celtic and with the game nearing its end had limited the score to 2–0 for the Portuguese side. [[Diamantino Costa|Diamantino]], however, scored directly from a free-kick in the final minute to level the tie on aggregate and force extra time. No further goals were scored in the additional 30 minutes, and the tie was decided by the [[Coin flipping|toss of a coin]]. Captain Billy McNeill successfully called, and Celtic progressed to the quarter-finals.<ref name="ec6970" /><ref name="cw250">{{harvnb|Campbell|Woods|1987|p=250}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=Memórias da Champions: a moeda que derrotou o Benfica em 1969|trans-title=Memories of the European Cup: the coin that defeated Benfica in 1969|url=http://www.maisfutebol.iol.pt/benfica/iol/733306-1456.html|work=Maisfutebol|date=17 October 2006|language=Portuguese|archive-date=21 June 2018|access-date=7 August 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180621221209/http://www.maisfutebol.iol.pt/benfica/iol/733306-1456.html|url-status=dead}}</ref> Celtic were unhappy that the tie had been decided in this manner, and suggested to UEFA an alternative of using a score line based on the number of corners forced in extra-time. UEFA rejected this proposal, but soon afterwards introduced [[Penalty shoot-out (association football)|penalty kick shoot-outs]] in the European Cup to decide drawn ties after extra-time.<ref name="ec6970" /> Celtic went on to face Italian champions [[ACF Fiorentina|Fiorentina]] in the quarter-finals, winning 3–1 on aggregate.<ref name="ec6970" /><ref name="cw250" />

The semi-finals saw Celtic drawn against English champions Leeds United. This was the first occasion that the reigning champions of England and Scotland had played each other in a fully competitive European tie.<ref name="cwlu1">{{harvnb|Campbell|Woods|1987|p=251}}</ref> The first leg took place at [[Elland Road]], with a goal in the opening minute from [[George Connelly]] giving Celtic a 1–0 lead to take back to Glasgow for the second leg.<ref name="ec6970" /><ref name="cwlu1" /> The return match was played at Hampden Park on 15 April 1970 in front of 136,505 spectators,<ref name="lu2">{{cite web|title=Matches 15 April 1970 - Celtic 2 Leeds United 1|url=http://www.mightyleeds.co.uk/matches/19700415.htm|website=The Mighty Mighty Whites|access-date=6 October 2013}}</ref> a record attendance for a competitive European club tie that stands to this day.<ref name="ec6970" /> In 14 minutes, [[Billy Bremner]] scored from long range to level the tie on aggregate.<ref name="lu2" /> Celtic kept their composure though and equalized two minutes into the second half though a John Hughes header.<ref name="lu2" /> Jimmy Johnstone had a particularly outstanding match<ref name="lu2" /><ref name="cwlu">{{harvnb|Campbell|Woods|1987|p=252}}</ref> and his mazy run set up Bobby Murdoch to score with a powerful shot,<ref name="cwlu" /> sealing a 2–1 win for Celtic on the night and their progression to the final.<ref name="lu2" /><ref name="cwlu" />

The [[1970 European Cup Final|final]] took place on 6 May 1970 at the [[San Siro]] in [[Milan]] against Dutch side [[Feyenoord]].<ref name="ec6970" /> Celtic were overwhelming favourites, but despite Tommy Gemmell opening the scoring after 30 minutes, they were comprehensively outplayed by the Dutch side. Feyenoord manager [[Ernst Happel]] ensured Jimmy Johnstone was double marked at all times, while the midfield trio of [[Franz Hasil]], [[Willem van Hanegem]] and [[Wim Jansen]] dominated their Celtic counterparts. [[Rinus Israël]] quickly equalized from a free kick, but Celtic managed to hold on at 1–1 to force extra time. With just two minutes of extra time remaining, a long free-kick from the Feyenoord half was sent towards the Celtic penalty area. Billy McNeill stumbled and misjudged the ball, and as he tried to recover he appeared to punch the ball away. Before the referee had a chance to award a penalty, [[Ove Kindvall]] reacted quickly and took the ball around his man before chipping it over goalkeeper [[Evan Williams (footballer)|Evan Williams]] to seal a 2–1 win for Feyenoord.<ref name="ec6970" /><ref>{{cite web|last=Brogan|first=Tom|title=Celtic 1970: European Cup Final v Feyenoord – The Dream Ends|url=http://stateofthegame.co.uk/2006/10/20/celtic-1970-european-cup-final-v-feyenoord-the-dream-ends/|website=State of the Game|access-date=26 July 2015|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080819150316/http://stateofthegame.co.uk/2006/10/20/celtic-1970-european-cup-final-v-feyenoord-the-dream-ends/|archive-date=19 August 2008}}</ref>

Celtic reached the semi-final of the European Cup in [[1971–72 European Cup|1972]], with Inter Milan as their opponents. The tie remained goal-less after the end of both games and 30 minutes of extra time in the second leg in Glasgow. As such, the tie went to [[Penalty shoot-out (association football)|penalty kicks]] to find a winner, only the second time a European Cup tie was decided in this manner. Inter Milan's Sandro Mazzola took the first penalty kick and scored. [[John Deans|Dixie Deans]] took the next penalty, Celtic's first, but fired his shot over the crossbar. The Italians scored all of their remaining penalties to eliminate Celtic.<ref>{{cite web|title=Season 1971–72|url=http://www.europeancuphistory.com/euro72.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070929083240/http://www.europeancuphistory.com/euro72.html|url-status=usurped|archive-date=29 September 2007|website=European Cup History|access-date=26 March 2014}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last=Jacobs|first=Raymond|title=Disappointing European defeat for Celtic on penalty kicks|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=Tus9AAAAIBAJ&pg=2293%2C3639884|access-date=7 June 2015|work=The Glasgow Herald|date=20 April 1972|page=4}}</ref>

Two years later in [[1973–74 European Cup|1974]], Celtic again made a European Cup semi-final, this time against [[Atlético Madrid]]. The tie against the Spaniards was acrimonious. Atlético were managed by [[Juan Carlos Lorenzo]], who had coached [[Argentina national football team|Argentina]] at the [[1966 FIFA World Cup]] where his players were branded "animals" by [[England national football team|England]] manager [[Alf Ramsey]].<ref name="ec7374">{{cite web|title=Season 1973–74|url=http://www.europeancuphistory.com/euro74.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070929083410/http://www.europeancuphistory.com/euro74.html|url-status=usurped|archive-date=29 September 2007|website=European Cup History|access-date=26 March 2014}}</ref><ref name="ath">{{harvnb|Campbell|Woods|1987|p=278}}</ref> In the first leg at Parkhead, the Atlético players continually kicked and hacked their opponents.<ref name="ec7374" /><ref>{{cite web|title=Atletico v Celtic 1974: why there's history|url=http://sport.stv.tv/football/clubs/celtic/283862-atletico-v-celtic-1974-why-theres-history/|work=STV Sport|access-date=6 October 2013|archive-date=12 November 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131112163954/http://sport.stv.tv/football/clubs/celtic/283862-atletico-v-celtic-1974-why-theres-history/|url-status=dead}}</ref> Three Atlético players were sent off, but their incessant foul play made it impossible, indeed physically dangerous, for Celtic to play their normal game.<ref name="ec7374" /><ref name="ath" /> The match finished 0–0,<ref name="ath" /> and the sour evening was completed with a punch-up between the two sets of players as they made their way up the tunnel at full-time.<ref name="ec7374" /><ref name="ath" /> In the buildup for the second leg in Spain, Jimmy Johnstone received a death threat over the hotel phone<ref name="ath" /> and a hate campaign from the Spanish media prevented the Celtic players from relaxing or training effectively.<ref name="ath" /> Atlético won the match 2–0, winning the tie on aggregate<ref name="ec7374" /><ref name="ath" /> and progressing to the [[1974 European Cup Final|final]] against [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]] where the Spanish side lost 4–0 after a replay.<ref name="ec7374" />

====Decline====
The mid-to-late 1970s saw a decline in Celtic's European achievements,<ref name="cie" /> with first-round exits in the European Cup against [[Olympiacos F.C.|Olympiacos]] in 1974<ref>{{cite news|last=Archer|first=Ian|title=Lennox sent off as Celtic go out|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=IZFAAAAAIBAJ&pg=3841%2C653742|access-date=7 December 2016|work=The Glasgow Herald|date=3 October 1974|page=4}}</ref> and in the [[UEFA Cup]] against [[Wisła Kraków]] in 1976.<ref>{{cite news|last=Reynolds|first=Jim|title=Celtic pole-axed|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=jd49AAAAIBAJ&pg=3849%2C6472081|access-date=7 December 2016|work=The Glasgow Herald|date=30 September 1976|page=20}}</ref> In between these very brief campaigns, Celtic reached the quarter-finals of the Cup Winners' Cup in season [[1975–76 European Cup Winners' Cup|1975–76]], but disappointingly were knocked out by [[East Germany|East German]] side [[FSV Zwickau|Sachsenring Zwickau]], whose play was unimpressive despite winning the tie.<ref>{{cite news|last=Taylor|first=Hugh|title=The day angry Celts drew a Blank|url=http://image.wikifoundry.com/image/3/9e3f6887cd7e5303c37144ba87e076ec/GW950H1260|access-date=7 December 2016|work=Daily Record|date=18 March 1976|page=38|archive-date=20 December 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161220093735/http://image.wikifoundry.com/image/3/9e3f6887cd7e5303c37144ba87e076ec/GW950H1260|url-status=dead}}</ref> The tie is best remembered for the bizarre situation where young [[Roy Aitken]] had to be 'adopted' by Celtic to be allowed to enter East Germany to play the away leg. At only 17 years old, the authorities there did not consider him to be an [[Age of majority|adult]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://homepage.ntlworld.com/k.tole/Past%20Bhoys/Individuals/Roy%20Aitken.PDF |title=Past Bhoys - Roy Aitken |access-date=29 January 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140311163950/http://homepage.ntlworld.com/k.tole/Past%20Bhoys/Individuals/Roy%20Aitken.PDF |archive-date=11 March 2014 |url-status=dead }}</ref> Celtic's last European campaign under Jock Stein was again very short, a second round exit against [[FC Wacker Innsbruck|Wacker Innsbruck]] in the [[1977–78 European Cup]].<ref>{{cite news|last=Reynolds|first=Jim|title=Lynch is sent off as Celtic crash|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=Q49AAAAAIBAJ&pg=3495%2C820457|access-date=7 December 2016|work=The Glasgow Herald|date=3 November 1977|page=20}}</ref> Celtic's failure to win any trophies that season, or finish high enough in the league, saw them fail to qualify for any of the European competitions in [[1978–79 Celtic F.C. season|season 1978–79]]; the first season Celtic had not competed in Europe since the early 1960s.<ref>{{cite news |last=Davidson |first=Alan |title=A dismal time |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=DAFBAAAAIBAJ&sjid=iacMAAAAIBAJ&pg=4312%2C160852 |access-date=17 August 2021 |work=Evening Times |date=1 May 1978}}</ref>

However, in [[1979–80 European Cup|1979–80]], under former player and new manager Billy McNeill, they reached the quarter-finals of the European Cup.<ref name="Campbell & Woods 1987 300">{{harvnb|Campbell|Woods|1987|p=300}}</ref> Celtic were drawn against [[Real Madrid C.F.|Real Madrid]], and goals from [[George McCluskey]] and [[Johnny Doyle (Scottish footballer)|Johnny Doyle]] gave Celtic a 2–0 win in the first leg at Parkhead.<ref name="rmwin">{{cite news|last=Archer|first=Ian|title=Wonderful! Late goals halt Real swagger|url=http://image.wikifoundry.com/image/1/dKb3gmKVJWr--PC5GVMndg855962/GW977H1292|access-date=27 January 2015|newspaper=Daily Express|date=6 March 1980|archive-date=10 June 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150610050029/http://image.wikifoundry.com/image/1/dKb3gmKVJWr--PC5GVMndg855962/GW977H1292|url-status=dead}}</ref> Goalkeeper [[Peter Latchford]] had an excellent match, and in particular his saves from England striker [[Laurie Cunningham]] and Spanish star [[Santillana (footballer)|Santillana]] were crucial in helping Celtic keep a [[Clean sheet#Association football|clean sheet]].<ref name="rmwin" /> The return leg took place two weeks later at the [[Santiago Bernabéu Stadium]] in front of approximately 120,000 fans. Johnny Doyle missed an excellent chance to score early in the first half, and then just before half-time Santillana pulled a goal back for Real. [[Uli Stielike]] levelled the tie in the second half with a powerful volley past Latchford, before [[Juanito (footballer, born 1954)|Juanito]] added a third near the end to send Celtic out 3–2 on aggregate.<ref name="Campbell & Woods 1987 300"/><ref>{{cite news|last=Archer|first=Ian|title=Out! Celtic crash to late goal|url=http://image.wikifoundry.com/image/1/KiqZdzHqEHsVexIdKXlQKQ848389/GW932H1206|access-date=26 November 2015|work=Daily Express|date=20 March 1980|archive-date=5 February 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150205025505/http://image.wikifoundry.com/image/1/KiqZdzHqEHsVexIdKXlQKQ848389/GW932H1206|url-status=dead}}</ref>

===1980s and 1990s===

====False starts====
[[File:Mcneill.jpg|thumb|left|Billy McNeill in September 1982]]Celtic's first European campaign of the decade ended inauspiciously in October 1980 when they were knocked out at the first round stage of the [[1980–81 European Cup Winners' Cup|European Cup Winners' Cup]] on away goals by Romanian side [[FC Politehnica Timișoara|Politehnica Timișoara]].<ref>{{cite news|last=Davidson|first=Alan|title=Celtic's sorry lesson|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=f_BAAAAAIBAJ&pg=6114%2C428254|access-date=9 June 2015|work=Evening Times|date=2 October 1980|page=38}}</ref> The [[1981–82 European Cup|following season]] saw the club take part in the European Cup. [[Murdo MacLeod]]'s left foot drive past [[Dino Zoff]] gave Celtic a 1–0 win in the first leg of the first-round tie against [[Juventus FC|Juventus]],<ref>{{cite news|last=Reynolds|first=Jim|title=MacLeod the Celtic hero|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=jMFAAAAAIBAJ&pg=2620%2C3548074|access-date=9 June 2015|work=The Glasgow Herald|date=17 September 1981|page=22}}</ref> however the Italians won the return match 2–0 in [[Turin]] to eliminate Celtic.<ref>{{cite news|last=Reynolds|first=Jim|title=Nightmare as Celtic fail to hold Juventus|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=tuw9AAAAIBAJ&pg=2744%2C169121|access-date=9 June 2015|work=The Glasgow Herald|date=1 October 1981|page=15}}</ref>

[[AFC Ajax|Ajax]] were Celtic's first-round European Cup opponents in September 1982. The first leg in Glasgow saw Ajax twice take the lead through goals from [[Jesper Olsen]] and [[Søren Lerby]], but Celtic responded with strikes from [[Charlie Nicholas]] and [[Frank McGarvey]] to draw 2–2 on the night.<ref>{{cite news|last=Reynolds|first=Jim|title=Cruyff-inspired Ajax old Celtic|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=VsNAAAAAIBAJ&pg=3806%2C3081708|access-date=9 June 2015|work=The Glasgow Herald|date=16 September 1982|page=18}}</ref> Celtic went into the second leg as underdogs, but an outstanding Charlie Nicholas goal and a last minute winner from George McCluskey saw the club progress 3–2 on aggregate.<ref>{{cite news|last=Reynolds|first=Jim|title=Late McCluskey goal takes Celtic through a European classic|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=YMNAAAAAIBAJ&pg=2443%2C5551851|access-date=9 June 2015|work=The Glasgow Herald|date=30 September 1982|page=20}}</ref> Nicholas has since described his goal in [[Amsterdam]] as being "probably my all-time favorite".<ref>{{cite news|title=Watch Charlie Nicholas score what he describes as his favourite goal EVER for Celtic against Ajax in 1982|url=http://www.dailyrecord.co.uk/sport/football/football-news/watch-charlie-nicholas-score-what-6458710|access-date=3 December 2015|work=Daily Record|date=17 September 2015}}</ref> Celtic were unable to keep up their winning form in the next round, and went out of Europe 3–2 on aggregate to [[Real Sociedad]], largely due to poor defending.<ref>{{cite news|last=Reynolds|first=Jim|title=Celtic's bravery is not quite enough|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=FcJAAAAAIBAJ&pg=2256%2C841020|access-date=9 June 2015|work=The Glasgow Herald|date=4 November 1982|page=18}}</ref>

The [[1983–84 UEFA Cup|1983–84 season]] saw Celtic competing in the [[UEFA Europa League|UEFA Cup]]. After a straightforward 5–1 aggregate win over [[Aarhus Gymnastikforening|AGF Aarhus]] in September 1983,<ref name="fbe8384">{{cite web |title=Results for Season 1983/1984 in UEFA Cup |url=http://www.fitbastats.com/celtic/team_results_season.php?from=29&competition=5&update=Update |work=FitbaStats}}</ref> Celtic were then drawn against [[Sporting Clube de Portugal|Sporting CP]] in the second round. The first leg away in Lisbon on 19 October saw Celtic start brightly but fade very quickly, and were fortunate to lose only 2–0. Manager [[Davie Hay]] admitted Celtic were "lucky" to not concede more goals, although he insisted that whilst they were in an "awkward position" to salvage the tie, it was not "impossible".<ref>{{cite news |work=Evening Times |date=20 October 1983 |title=We were lucky: Hay admits it could have been a disaster |first=Chick |last=Young |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=PtBAAAAAIBAJ&pg=5820%2C3879934}}</ref> Celtic's response two weeks later in the return match in Glasgow saw them produce a performance described by ''[[The Herald (Glasgow)|The Herald]]'' as "quite brilliant". [[Tommy Burns (footballer)|Tommy Burns]] ran the show for Celtic and opened the scoring on 17 minutes with a headed goal. Shortly before half-time, [[Tom McAdam]] levelled the tie with a powerful shot. On half-time, [[Brian McClair]] took a pass from Frank McGarvey, ran 30 yards before shooting in to put Celtic ahead on aggregate. Further goals in the second half from Murdo MacLeod and McGarvey sealed an emphatic 5–0 win on the night and progression to the third round.<ref>{{cite news|last=Paul|first=Ian|title=Celtic treat fans to a vintage display|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=Lfs9AAAAIBAJ&pg=5997%2C658796|access-date=21 June 2016|work=The Glasgow Herald|date=3 November 1983}}</ref> Having replaced Billy McNeill as manager in the summer, Davie Hay described the win as "the best performance since I took over".<ref>{{cite news|last=Young|first=Chick|title=Celtic want Spurs|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=X9BAAAAAIBAJ&pg=5961%2C554092|access-date=21 June 2016|work=Evening Times|date=3 November 1983}}</ref> Celtic's next opponents were [[Nottingham Forest F.C.|Nottingham Forest]],<ref name="fbe8384" /> and a scoreless draw away in the first leg put them in a strong position. Celtic dominated the match, with Forest manager [[Brian Clough]] acknowledging that Celtic "played really well" and had been "the best team on the night".<ref>{{cite news|last=Davidson|first=Alan|title=Treble Scotch|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=cdBAAAAAIBAJ&pg=5850%2C4678633|access-date=21 June 2016|work=Evening Times|date=24 November 1983}}</ref> The second leg, however, proved to be an anti-climax for Celtic as Forest outplayed them, easing to a 2–1 win on the night to eliminate the Scots.<ref>{{cite news|last=Davidson|first=Alan|title=A wrong turning|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=8NZAAAAAIBAJ&pg=6016%2C1782143|access-date=21 June 2016|work=Evening Times|date=8 December 1983}}</ref>

====The Rapid Vienna affair and fall-out====
Celtic began their [[1984–85 European Cup Winners' Cup]] campaign against [[Belgium|Belgian]] side [[K.A.A. Gent]]. A 1–0 loss away in the first leg, was recovered comfortably in the return match at Parkhead. Frank McGarvey levelled the tie before half-time with a close-range shot after the Belgian goalkeeper failed to hold onto a Brian McClair shot. McGarvey put Celtic ahead with another goal early in the second half, before Paul McStay sealed a 3–0 win with a header on 89 minutes.<ref>{{cite news|last=Cameron|first=Alex|title=McGarvey is Celts ace|url=http://image.wikifoundry.com/image/3/010266151ae7272028466501f523b9fb/GW894H984|access-date=9 December 2016|work=Daily Record|date=4 October 1984|archive-date=20 December 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161220093925/http://image.wikifoundry.com/image/3/010266151ae7272028466501f523b9fb/GW894H984|url-status=dead}}</ref>

Celtic then found themselves embroiled in a series of controversial matches in the second round against [[SK Rapid Wien|Rapid Wien]]. Celtic lost the first leg 1–3 in [[Vienna]], but despite rough-house tactics from the Austrians, it was only Celtic's [[Alan McInally]] who found himself red-carded.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?nid=2507&dat=19841025&id=eMFAAAAAIBAJ&pg=3530,5861209 |newspaper=The Glasgow Herald |date=25 October 1984 |page=20 |title=Celtic bruised but not yet beaten |first=Ian |last=Paul |access-date=9 January 2015}}</ref> The return match at Parkhead was an even more bad-tempered affair as Celtic raced to a 3–0 lead with goals from Brian McClair, Murdo MacLeod and Tommy Burns, all in spite of Rapid's foul play. The match erupted near the end when Burns was punched by [[Reinhard Kienast]]. In the ensuing chaos, coins and at least one bottle were thrown onto the pitch by some Celtic fans. None appeared to hit anybody, but one of the Rapid Wien players, [[Rudi Weinhofer]], was carried off the pitch with his head swathed in bandages. The match finished 3–0, with Celtic winning the tie 4–3 on aggregate.<ref name="rv1">{{cite news |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?nid=2507&dat=19841108&id=lMBAAAAAIBAJ&pg=3791,1776989 |newspaper=The Glasgow Herald |date=8 November 1984 |page=22 |title=Ugly scenes mar memorable Celtic victory |last=Paul |first=Ian |access-date=9 January 2015}}</ref>

Rapid, however, appealed, citing the alleged injury to their player. The initial appeal was dismissed by UEFA, but a second appeal from the club was upheld and a [[Replay (sports)#Association football|replay]] was ordered to take place at least 150 kilometres (90 miles) from Glasgow.<ref>{{cite news|last=Reynolds|first=Jim|title=Celtic ordered to reply match|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=sfM9AAAAIBAJ&pg=3475%2C5328910|access-date=8 July 2014|newspaper=The Glasgow Herald|date=24 November 1984|page=1}}</ref> The Celtic board acquiesced to this decision in spite of considered opinion that Celtic should withdraw from the tournament on principle.<ref>{{harvnb|Campbell|Woods|1987|pp=329–330}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|last=Reynolds|first=Jim|title=We should fight Europe together|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=pcBAAAAAIBAJ&pg=2498%2C6419619|newspaper=The Glasgow Herald|access-date=30 January 2015|page=26|date=28 November 1984}}</ref> As such, the match at Parkhead was declared void and a third match was held in [[Manchester]] at [[Old Trafford]]. Celtic lost 0–1, the goal coming on 18 minutes when after [[Roy Aitken]] had hit the post with an effort for Celtic, Rapid immediately caught Celtic on the break to score the only goal of the game. Worse, two Celtic fans assaulted Rapid Wien players; one fan ran onto the pitch and accosted the goalkeeper during the game, whilst at full-time another Rapid player was kicked by a fan.<ref name="rv2">{{cite news |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?nid=2507&dat=19841213&id=0rZAAAAAIBAJ&pg=3980,2726282 |newspaper=The Glasgow Herald |page=20 |title=Disaster night for Scots clubs in Europe |last=Paul |first=Ian |date=13 December 1984 |access-date=9 January 2015}}</ref> UEFA fined Celtic and ordered their next home European tie to take place behind closed doors.<ref>{{harvnb|Campbell|Woods|1987|p=331}}</ref>

The repercussions from the Rapid Wien ties followed Celtic into the [[1985–86 European Cup Winners' Cup]] campaign. The first round saw them pitted against old foes Atlético Madrid. [[Mo Johnston]]'s headed goal from a [[David Provan (footballer, born 1956)|Davie Provan]] cross and [[Pat Bonner]]'s late penalty save from Juan Jose Rubio secured a 1–1 draw away in [[Madrid]], putting Celtic in a good position to win the tie.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=4sBAAAAAIBAJ&pg=4427%2C4558943 |work=The Glasgow Herald |date=19 September 1985 |page=22 |title=Bonner saves penalty and Johnston snatches a priceless goal for Celtic |access-date=1 January 2014 |first=Ian |last=Paul}}</ref> The second leg in Glasgow, however, was played in front of an empty stadium as punishment for the fans' behaviour the previous season in the replayed game against Rapid. In the unreal atmosphere of playing behind closed doors, Celtic toiled and lost 2–1 for another early European exit.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=tsJAAAAAIBAJ&pg=4802%2C888447 |work=The Glasgow Herald |date=3 October 1985 |page=20 |title=Celtic decide to go quietly |access-date=1 January 2014 |first=Ian |last=Paul}}</ref>

====Years in the wilderness====
The club's League title win in 1986 saw them qualify for the European Cup in [[1986–87 European Cup|1986–87]], where Celtic eliminated [[Shamrock Rovers F.C.|Shamrock Rovers]] 3–0 on aggregate in the first round.<ref>{{cite web|title=Results for Season 1986/1987 in European Champions Cup|url=http://www.fitbastats.com/celtic/team_results_season.php?from=26&competition=8&update=Update|website=FitbaStats|access-date=22 June 2016}}</ref> The next round saw Celtic take on [[Soviet Union|Soviet]] champions [[Dynamo Kyiv]], a side comprising a large number of players in their [[Soviet Union national football team|national side]].<ref>{{cite news|last=Reynolds|first=Jim|title=Celtic draw an Iron Curtain test|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=C4JDAAAAIBAJ&pg=4313%2C1110898|access-date=18 March 2017|work=The Glasgow Herald|date=20 October 1986|page=20}}</ref> A hard-fought 1–1 draw in the first leg at Parkhead did not bode well for the return match in [[Kiev]].<ref>{{cite news|last=Reynolds|first=Jim|title=Johnston gives Celts a lifeline|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=GYJDAAAAIBAJ&pg=3210%2C5838990|access-date=18 March 2017|work=The Glasgow Herald|date=23 October 1986|page=20}}</ref> [[Oleh Blokhin]]'s free-kick in the second leg gave the Soviet's an early lead, but [[Mark McGhee]] levelled the tie shortly after half-time. Dynamo regained the lead later on in the second half, but Celtic continued to press. A late third goal from Dynamo, however, saw them clinch the tie.<ref>{{cite news|last=Cameron|first=Alex|title=Celtic go down but not in shame|url=http://image.wikifoundry.com/image/1/qhhJhyasG1eiuQQI_2ECNA855709/GW661H1628|access-date=22 June 2016|work=Daily Record|date=6 November 1986|archive-date=20 April 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160420040654/http://image.wikifoundry.com/image/1/qhhJhyasG1eiuQQI_2ECNA855709/GW661H1628|url-status=dead}}</ref>

Early exits in Europe continued as the decade progressed. [[Borussia Dortmund]] eliminated Celtic in the first round of the UEFA Cup in [[1987–88 UEFA Cup|1987]],<ref>{{cite news|last=Young|first=Chick|title=A case for the defence|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=8thAAAAAIBAJ&pg=3979%2C7648409|access-date=18 March 2017|work=Evening Times|date=30 September 1987|pages=42–43}}</ref> while [[SV Werder Bremen|Werder Bremen]] eliminated Celtic from the European Cup at the second round stage in [[1988–89 European Cup|1988]].<ref>{{cite web|title=Results For Season 1988/1989 in European Champions Cup|url=http://www.fitbastats.com/celtic/team_results_season.php?from=24&competition=8&update=Update|website=FitbaStats|access-date=18 March 2017}}</ref> In September 1989, Celtic's new [[Poland|Polish]] striker [[Dariusz Dziekanowski]] scored four goals at Parkhead in the second leg of a first round European Cup Winners Cup encounter with [[FK Partizan|Partizan Belgrade]], but the team still went out of the competition under the [[away goals rule]].<ref>{{cite news |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?nid=2507&dat=19890928&id=fjFAAAAAIBAJ&pg=3897,3810410 |newspaper=The Glasgow Herald |date=28 September 1989 |page=28 |title=Dziekanowski's four goals are not enough |last=Paul |first=Ian |access-date=9 January 2015}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.eveningtimes.co.uk/other-football/now-you-know-jackies-four-goal-spree-not-enough-for-celtic-108487n.19541590 |title=NOW YOU KNOW: Jackie's four-goal spree not enough for Celtic |newspaper=Evening Times |date=29 November 2012 |access-date=5 August 2013}}</ref> Celtic were very poor that season, and failed to qualify for any of the European competitions in 1990–91 for the first time in 12 years.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Morrison |first1=Andrew |last2=Panton |first2=Malcolm |title=On this day - 12th May 1990 |url=https://www.afc.co.uk/2020/05/12/on-this-day-12th-may-1990/ |website=Aberdeen Football Club}}</ref>

Celtic were back in Europe for the [[1991–92 UEFA Cup]] under new manager [[Liam Brady]], but lost 5–1 in the first leg of the first round to Swiss side [[Neuchâtel Xamax]] as Egyptian striker [[Hossam Hassan]] ran amok against the Celtic defense by scoring four goals,<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.worldfootball.net/match-report/ma610807/lineup/ |title=Match details / line-up: Neuchâtel Xamax FC - Celtic FC (Europa League 1991/1992) |website=Worldfootball.net |access-date=5 August 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://thecelticblog.com/2012/04/blogs/in-my-memory-neuchatel-xamax-v-celtic-uefa-cup-92 |title=In My Memory: Neuchatel Xamex v Celtic UEFA Cup 92 |website=The Celtic Blog |date=4 April 2012 |access-date=5 August 2013}}</ref> one of the worst European defeats in the club's history. In the return leg at Celtic Park, Celtic could only manage a 1–0 victory and crashed out of the tournament 5–2 on aggregate.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.worldfootball.net/match-report/ma610808/lineup/ |title=Match details / line-up: Celtic FC - Neuchâtel Xamax FC (Europa League 1991/1992) |website=Worldfootball.net |access-date=5 August 2013}}</ref>

The following season, Liam Brady was struggling as Celtic manager, but enjoyed a brief respite in the first round of the [[1992–93 UEFA Cup|UEFA Cup]]. Celtic recovered from a 2–0 first leg defeat away at [[1. FC Köln|Cologne]] in the [[1992–93 UEFA Cup|UEFA Cup]] to win the return match in Glasgow 3–0, midfielder [[John Collins (footballer, born 1968)|John Collins]] scoring the decisive third goal seven minutes from time.<ref name="s9293-2">{{cite web |url=http://www.ntvcelticfanzine.com/the%2090s/90s9293pt2.htm |title=Don't look back in anger - Celtic in the 90s (season 92-93 part 2) |website=Not the View |access-date=5 August 2013 |archive-date=29 October 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131029201935/http://www.ntvcelticfanzine.com/the%2090s/90s9293pt2.htm |url-status=dead }}</ref> Celtic were drawn against another German side in the next round, Borussia Dortmund. In the first leg away in [[Dortmund]], Celtic turned in a good performance; Paul McStay and John Collins constantly troubled the Dortmund defence. Celtic came close to scoring after hitting the crossbar, and were unlucky to lose 1–0. [[Gerry Creaney]] levelled the tie on aggregate with a first-half goal in Glasgow. Dortmund, however, took control in the second half, scored twice and sent Celtic out 3–1 on aggregate.<ref name="s9293-2" /><ref>{{cite web|title=UEFA Cup 1992/1993 » 2. Round|url=https://www.worldfootball.net/competition/ro10175/md0/results-and-standings/|website=Wordfootball.net|access-date=4 June 2015}}</ref>

Celtic again featured in the UEFA Cup in season 1993–94, knocking out [[BSC Young Boys|Young Boys Bern]] 1–0 on aggregate, with the second leg at Celtic Park going to extra time. The winning goal was an own goal by Berne's [[Alain Baumann]], and the match itself is more remembered for stadium announcer [[Tiger Tim Stevens|"Tiger" Tim Stevens]]' mocking comment at half-time about [[Rangers F.C.|Rangers]] being knocked out of European competition the same evening (he asked for a "minute's silence"): He was in turn sacked on the spot by the Celtic board of directors.<ref>{{cite news |last=Aitken |first=Mike |title=Own goal is enough to see Celtic through |url=https://www.thecelticwiki.com/1993-09-29-celtic-1-0-young-boys-berne-uefa-cup/ |access-date=17 August 2021 |work=The Scotsman |date=30 September 1993}}</ref> The second round saw Celtic drawn against [[Sporting CP]]. A credible performance at home in the first leg resulted in a 1–0 win, Creaney controlling a [[Paul Byrne (footballer, born 1972)|Paul Byrne]] pass with his chest on the edge of the penalty area and scoring with a powerful shot.<ref>{{cite news |last=McKinney |first=David |title=Celtic 1 Sporting Lisbon 0 |url=https://www.thecelticwiki.com/1993-10-20-celtic-1-0-sporting-lisbon-uefa-cup/ |access-date=17 August 2021 |work=The Independent |date=21 October 1993}}</ref> But the Portuguese side won 2–0 in the return match, to knock Celtic out.<ref>{{cite news |title=Cadete's double destroys Celtic |url=https://www.thecelticwiki.com/1993-11-03-sporting-lisbon-2-0-celtic-uefa-cup/ |access-date=17 August 2021 |work=The Times |date=4 November 1993}}</ref>

Celtic failed to qualify for Europe in season 1994–95, but their [[1995 Scottish Cup Final|Scottish Cup Final win in 1995]] under new manager [[Tommy Burns (footballer)|Tommy Burns]] (the club's first trophy in six years) saw them return to European competition the following season in the [[European Cup Winner's Cup]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=English |first1=Tom |title=Celtic triumph with Dutch courage |url=https://www.thecelticwiki.com/1995-05-27-celtic-1-0-airdrieonians-scottish-cup-final/ |access-date=17 August 2021 |work=The Sunday Times |date=28 May 1995}}</ref> [[Georgia (country)|Georgia]]n side, [[FC Dinamo Batumi|Dinamo Batumi]], were beaten 7–2 on aggregate in the first round.<ref>{{cite news |last1=McCarra |first1=Kevin |title=Celtic youngsters draw inspiration from Thom |url=https://www.thecelticwiki.com/1995-09-28-celtic-4-0-dinamo-batumi-ecwc/ |access-date=17 August 2021 |work=The Times |date=29 September 1995}}</ref> [[Paris Saint-Germain F.C.|Paris Saint-Germain]] were the second round opponents, and a narrow 1–0 loss away in Paris kept hopes high that Celtic could turn the tie around at Celtic Park.<ref>{{cite news |title=Djorkaeff checks Celtic's progress |url=https://www.thecelticwiki.com/1995-10-19-paris-st-germain-1-0-celtic-ecwc/ |access-date=17 August 2021 |work=The Independent |date=20 October 1995}}</ref> However, despite a bright start, Celtic ended up being comprehensively outclassed. Two [[Patrice Loko]] goals for PSG in the first half put the tie beyond Celtic, with [[Pascal Nouma]] adding a third in the second half. Such was the quality of the French side's football, the Celtic supporters gave them a standing ovation at full-time.<ref>{{cite news |last=Moore |first=Glenn |title=Celtic defeat completes bleak week |url=https://www.thecelticwiki.com/1995-11-02-celtic-0-3-paris-st-germain-ecwc/ |access-date=17 August 2021 |work=The Independent |date=3 November 1995}}</ref> PSG would go on to win the Cup Winner's Cup that season, defeating [[Rapid Vienna]] in the final<ref>{{cite news |title=NOW YOU KNOW: PSG defeated Celtic twice on way to winning European Cup-Winners Cup in 1996 |url=https://www.glasgowtimes.co.uk/sport/other_football/14982157.now-you-know-psg-defeated-celtic-twice-on-way-to-winning-european-cup-winners-cup-in-1996/ |access-date=17 August 2021 |work=Glasgow Times |date=22 December 2016}}</ref> Celtic returned to the UEFA Cup for the following season, but they did not progress far: They defeated [[FC VSS Košice|Košice]] 1–0 on aggregate in a qualifying round, then lost 2–0 in both legs of the first round proper to [[Hamburger SV]] to go out 4–0 on aggregate.<ref>{{cite web |title=Results For Season 1996/1997 in UEFA Cup |url=https://www.fitbastats.com/celtic/team_results_season.php?from=16&competition=5&update=Update |website=FitbaStats |access-date=17 August 2021}}</ref>

Under new Head Coach [[Wim Jansen]], Celtic had to negotiate two qualifying rounds to qualify for the first round proper of the [[1997–98 UEFA Cup]]. Welsh [[Cardiff Metropolitan University F.C.|Inter Cabel-Tel]] were comfortably disposed of 8–0 on aggregate,<ref>{{cite news|title=Celtic cruise past Cardiff part-timers|url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football-celtic-cruise-past-cardiff-part-timers-1253330.html|access-date=28 December 2016|work=The Independent|date=29 July 1997}}</ref> but the next qualifying round against [[FC Tirol Innsbruck|Tirol Innsbruck]] proved more difficult. An abysmal first-leg performance in Austria was salvaged only by a late [[Alan Stubbs]] free kick, Celtic losing 2–1 on the night but netting a valuable away goal.<ref>{{cite news|title=Stubbs rescues Celtic|url=http://www.thecelticwiki.com/page/1997-07-23%3A+Inter+CableTel+0-3+Celtic%2C+UEFA+Cup+1st+Qual.+Rd.|access-date=28 December 2016|work=The Scotsman|date=13 August 1997}}</ref> The return match in Glasgow was an exciting see-saw affair. Celtic twice took the lead in the first half, only for Innsbruck to equalise on each occasion, their second goal an own goal by [[Henrik Larsson]] just before half-time. Goals in the second half from [[Simon Donnelly]] and [[Craig Burley]] put Celtic ahead on aggregate, but [[Gernot Krinner]]'s header on 82 minutes levelled the tie on aggregate and gave the Austrians the advantage because of the away goals rule. But Celtic responded with three minutes to go, Wieghorst showed excellent skill in the penalty box to control Donnelly's knockdown before shooting in. With the match going into injury time and the tie poised on a knife edge, Larsson went on a solo run forward, beating several tackles, before passing to Burley who scored from close range to clinch a 6–3 win on the night to progress on aggregate.<ref>{{cite news |title=Celtic in fantasy land |url=https://www.thecelticwiki.com/1997-08-26-celtic-6-3-fc-tirol-innsbruck-uefa-cup-2nd-qualifier/ |access-date=17 August 2021 |work=The Scotsman |date=27 August 1997}}</ref> Celtic were then drawn against [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]] in the first round proper.

Celtic went into the first leg in Glasgow as massive underdogs, and the match seemed to be going as expected when 17-year-old [[Michael Owen]] put the English side ahead on 6 minutes. Celtic continued to struggle during the opening 20 minutes but began to find form as the first half progressed. The start of the second half saw Celtic pushing forward more and they equalized on 53 minutes. [[Jackie McNamara]] raced down the right wing, played a one-two with Craig Burley, then volleyed a powerful shot past [[David James (footballer, born 1970)|David James]]. Simon Donnelly hit the post on 62 minutes, then a minute later the referee ignored what appeared to be a blatant foul on him in the penalty box. Celtic continued to press forward, and on 73 minutes were awarded a penalty when Larsson fell on being challenged by David James in the penalty box. Donnelly converted the resultant spot kick to put Celtic ahead. With full-time approaching, [[Steve McManaman]] picked the ball from inside his own half, dribbled 70 yards to the edge of the Celtic penalty box and curled a shot to level the match at 2–2.<ref>{{cite news|last=Baillie|first=Rodger|title=The Scouse down|url=http://image.wikifoundry.com/image/3/c5da5578ab48a9c54206dc315c2411ab/GW1024H1325|access-date=28 December 2016|work=The Sun|date=17 September 1997|page=47|archive-date=29 December 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161229033056/http://image.wikifoundry.com/image/3/c5da5578ab48a9c54206dc315c2411ab/GW1024H1325|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last=Grahame|first=Ewing|title=A Brit of all right|url=http://www.thecelticwiki.com/page/1997-09-16%3A+Celtic+2-2+Liverpool%2C+UEFA+Cup+1st+Rd|access-date=28 December 2016|work=Daily Record|date=17 September 1997|pages=42–43}}</ref> The return match at [[Anfield]] produced fewer chances and no goals, seeing Liverpool knock out Celtic on away goals.<ref>{{cite news|last=Samuel|first=Martin|title=Reds edge fast show|url=http://image.wikifoundry.com/image/3/c5da5578ab48a9c54206dc315c2411ab/GW1024H1325|access-date=28 December 2016|work=The Express|date=1 October 1997|archive-date=29 December 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161229033056/http://image.wikifoundry.com/image/3/c5da5578ab48a9c54206dc315c2411ab/GW1024H1325|url-status=dead}}</ref>

Celtic's league title win in 1998 gave the club their first opportunity to compete in the rebranded [[UEFA Champions League]] in [[1998–99 UEFA Champions League|1998–99]]. Two qualifying rounds had first to be negotiated, however. [[St Patrick's Athletic F.C.|St Patrick's Athletic]] were knocked out,<ref>{{cite news|last=McLeman|first=Neil|title=Celtic clear first hurdle but have long way to go|url=http://www.thecelticwiki.com/page/1998-07-29%3A+St+Patrick%E2%80%99s+Athletic+0-2+Celtic,+Champions+League+Qualifying+1st+Rd|access-date=12 October 2015|work=The Scotsman|date=30 July 1998|archive-date=16 February 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160216065402/http://www.thecelticwiki.com/page/1998-07-29%3A+St+Patrick%E2%80%99s+Athletic+0-2+Celtic%2C+Champions+League+Qualifying+1st+Rd|url-status=dead}}</ref> but Celtic were then paired up against a strong [[GNK Dinamo Zagreb|Croatia Zagreb]] side comprising several players who had helped [[Croatia national football team|Croatia]] to third place at the [[1998 FIFA World Cup|1998 World Cup]].<ref name="bonus">{{cite news|last=Buckland|first=Simon|title=Little charity in Celtic's bonus row|url=http://www.independent.ie/sport/little-charity-in-celtics-bonus-row-26177142.html|access-date=12 October 2015|work=The Independent|date=12 August 1998|location=Ireland}}</ref> The first leg took place in Glasgow amidst a row between Celtic players and the club bosses over bonuses.<ref name="bonus" /> A [[Darren Jackson (Scottish footballer)|Darren Jackson]] goal gave Celtic a 1–0 win to take over to Zagreb,<ref>{{cite news|title=Jackson's win bonus as Celtic show no charity|url=http://www.thecelticwiki.com/page/1998-08-12%3A+Celtic+1-0+Croatia+Zagreb%2C+Champions+League+Qualifying+2nd+Rd|access-date=12 October 2015|work=The Scotsman|date=13 August 1998}}</ref> but an insipid second leg performance in match dominated by [[Robert Prosinečki]] saw Celtic beaten 3–0 to go out on aggregate. Only a fine showing from [[Jonathan Gould (goalkeeper)|Jonathan Gould]] in goal saved Celtic from a more emphatic defeat.<ref>{{cite news|last=Mackie|first=Fraser|title=Prosinecki supreme as Celtic take a pounding|url=http://www.thecelticwiki.com/page/1998-08-26%3A+Croatia+Zagreb+3-0+Celtic,+Champions+League+Qualifying+2nd+Rd|access-date=12 October 2015|work=The Scotsman|date=27 August 1998}}</ref> The club dropped into the [[1998–99 UEFA Cup|UEFA Cup]]. Portuguese side [[Vitória S.C.|Vitória de Guimarães]] were defeated 2–1 in both legs in the first round, but Celtic crashed out in the second round against Zürich 5–3 on aggregate<ref>{{cite news|last=Lappin|first=Tom|title=Celtic left with no defence over classic European exit|url=http://www.thecelticwiki.com/page/1998-11-03%3A+FC+Zurich+4-2+Celtic,+UEFA+Cup+2nd+Round|access-date=12 October 2015|work=The Scotsman|date=4 November 1998}}</ref> as a public row between captain [[Paul Lambert]] and managing director [[Jock Brown]] raged behind the scenes.<ref>{{cite news|last=Sinclair|first=Paul|title=Lambert and Brown bury the hatchet|url=http://www.thefreelibrary.com/Lambert+and+Brown+bury+the+hatchet.-a060940736|access-date=12 October 2015|work=Daily Record|date=6 November 1998}}</ref>

===2000–2010===

====Regaining respect====
New Celtic manager [[Martin O'Neill]]'s first European tie was a qualifying tie for the [[2000–01 UEFA Cup|UEFA Cup]] against [[Jeunesse Esch]] in August 2000. Having won the first game in Luxembourg 4–0, Celtic went on to ease to a 7–0 win in the return match in Glasgow. The match is notable for [[Mark Burchill]]'s three goals scored in the space of three minutes, the quickest hat-trick in the history of a UEFA club competition.<ref>{{cite web|last=Kerr|first=Thomas|title=Burchill's hurricane hat-trick|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2000/aug/25/newsstory.sport5|website=The Guardian|access-date=9 June 2015|date=25 August 2000}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|last=Conaghan|first=Martin|title=Timing is everything|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/scot_prem/8432401.stm|website=[[BBC Sport]]|access-date=9 June 2015|date=28 December 2009}}</ref> Celtic went on to eliminate [[Helsingin Jalkapalloklubi|HJK Helsinki]] in the first round proper,<ref>{{cite news|title=Sutton is Celtic's saviour|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/uefa_cup/945675.stm|access-date=28 December 2015|work=[[BBC Sport]]|date=28 September 2000}}</ref> before going out 3–2 on aggregate in the next round to [[FC Girondins de Bordeaux|Bordeaux]].<ref>{{cite news|title=UEFA Cup agony for Celtic|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/teams/c/celtic/1015978.stm|access-date=28 December 2015|work=[[BBC Sport]]|date=9 November 2000}}</ref>

Having won the league title in 2001, Martin O'Neill's side then played Ajax in the Netherlands in the first leg of the qualifier tie for the [[2001–02 UEFA Champions League|Champions League]] on 8 August 2001. Celtic turned in an outstanding performance.<ref name="Tictactic.co.uk">{{cite web|url=http://www.tictactic.co.uk/classic-tictactic-champions-league-3rd-qualifying-round-2001-ajax-1-3-celtic/ |title=Classic TicTacTic – Champions League 3rd Qualifying Round 2001: Ajax 1-3 Celtic |website=Tictactic.co.uk |date=11 July 2011 |access-date=6 August 2013 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140201215900/http://www.tictactic.co.uk/classic-tictactic-champions-league-3rd-qualifying-round-2001-ajax-1-3-celtic/ |archive-date=1 February 2014 }}</ref> First-half goals from Dutch winger [[Bobby Petta]] and [[Didier Agathe]] gave the Scottish champions a 2–1 lead at half-time. [[Chris Sutton]] added a third in the second half with a powerful header to give Celtic an impressive 3–1 win.<ref name="Forsyth">{{cite news|last=Forsyth |first=Roddy |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/european/3010365/Champions-League-Petta-sets-Celtic-on-the-path-to-glittering-prize.html |title=Champions League: Petta sets Celtic on the path to glittering prize |work=The Telegraph |date=8 August 2001 |access-date=6 August 2013}}</ref> A nervy second leg performance at Parkhead saw Celtic lose 0–1 on the night but progress to the group stages on aggregate; their first involvement in the Champions League group stages since the redevelopment of the tournament in the 1990s.<ref name="telegraph.co.uk">{{cite news |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/competitions/scottish-premier/3011251/Champions-League-Celtic-edge-through-but-nerves-show.html |title=Champions League: Celtic edge through but nerves show |work=The Telegraph |date=22 August 2001 |access-date=6 August 2013}}</ref>

Celtic made their debut in the group stages of the Champions League on 18 September 2001, losing 2–3 to Juventus in Turin. Celtic had recovered from a 2–0 deficit to level the match at 2–2, but lost a third goal near the end from a controversial penalty.<ref>{{cite news|first=Barry|last=Glendenning |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2001/sep/18/minutebyminute.sport1 |title=Juventus 3 - 2 Celtic |work=The Guardian |date=18 September 2001 |access-date=6 August 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.talksport.co.uk/magazine/virals/130211/martin-oneills-memorable-rant-celtics-clash-juventus-2001-191434 |title=Magazine: Martin O'Neill's memorable rant from Celtic's clash with Juventus in 2001 |website=Talksport.co.uk |access-date=6 August 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130214020117/http://www.talksport.co.uk/magazine/virals/130211/martin-oneills-memorable-rant-celtics-clash-juventus-2001-191434 |archive-date=14 February 2013 |url-status=dead }}</ref> Celtic recovered from that set-back to win their next two home matches against [[FC Porto|Porto]] and [[Rosenborg BK|Rosenborg]],<ref name="guardian.co.uk">{{cite news|first=Michael|last=Walker |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2001/sep/26/championsleague.porto |title=Celtic 1 - 0 Porto |work=The Guardian |date=26 September 2001 |access-date=6 August 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|first=Graham|last=Clark |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2001/oct/11/championsleague.sport |title=Celtic 1 - 0 Rosenborg |work=The Guardian |date=11 October 2001 |access-date=6 August 2013}}</ref> but lost the away matches against the same opposition.<ref>{{cite news|first=Daniel|last=Taylor |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2001/oct/18/championsleague.porto |title=Porto 3 - 0 Celtic|work=The Guardian |date=18 October 2001 |access-date=6 August 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|first=Michael|last=Walkerv|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2001/oct/24/championsleague.sport1 |title=Rosenborg 2 - 0 Celtic |work=The Guardian |date=24 October 2001 |access-date=6 August 2013}}</ref> Celtic's final group match was against Juventus at Parkhead on 31 October 2001. A Chris Sutton brace and an outstanding performance from [[Ľubomír Moravčík]] helped Celtic to a famous 4–3 win. Despite finishing the group with nine points, however, Celtic failed to qualify for the next round<ref name="news.bbc.co.uk">{{cite news|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/champions_league/1627972.stm |title=Celtic bow out in style |work=[[BBC Sport]] |date=31 October 2001 |access-date=6 August 2013}}</ref> and were demoted to the [[2001–02 UEFA Cup|UEFA Cup]]. They were drawn against Valencia, where in the first leg in Spain, the club had goalkeeper [[Rab Douglas]] to thank for keeping the score to a manageable 0–1 defeat.<ref>{{cite news|date=22 November 2001|title=Valencia vanquish Celtic|work=[[BBC Sport]]|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/teams/c/celtic/1666912.stm|access-date=6 August 2013}}</ref> The second leg in Glasgow saw [[Henrik Larsson]] scoring to give Celtic a 1–0 win after 90 minutes to level the tie on aggregate. As no goals were scored during extra-time, the tie went to penalty-kicks, where Celtic lost, suffering elimination.<ref name="ReferenceA">{{cite news|first=Graham|last=Clark|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2001/dec/07/uefa.sport |title=Uefa Cup: Celtic 1 - 0 Valencia (1-1 agg, Valencia win 5-4 on pens) |work=The Guardian |date=7 December 2001 |access-date=6 August 2013}}</ref>

====Impact in Europe====
Celtic's [[The Bhoys from Seville|European campaign in 2002–03]] began somewhat inauspiciously when they were beaten by Basel on away goals in a qualifier tie for the [[2002–03 UEFA Champions League|Champions League]].<ref>{{cite news|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/champions_league/2218965.stm |title=Celtic beaten in Basel |work=[[BBC Sport]] |date=28 August 2002 |access-date=6 August 2013}}</ref> Celtic dropped into the [[2002–03 UEFA Cup|UEFA Cup]], and in the first round eased to a 10–1 aggregate win over Lithuanian minnows [[FK Sūduva]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.thefreelibrary.com/FOOTBALL+%3A+CELTS+GO+ON+A+BALTIC+CRUISE%3B+So+easy+as+clever+Bhoys+make...-a092432444 |title=Celtic go on a Baltic cruise; So easy as clever Bhoys make it 10 out of 10 for the clever Bhoys|work=Daily Record |date=4 October 2002 |access-date=6 August 2013}}</ref> In the next round, Celtic were paired with [[Blackburn Rovers F.C.|Blackburn Rovers]],<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.redandwhitekop.com/forum/index.php?topic=7379.0 |title=UEFA Cup draw. Celtic v Blackburn |website=Redandwhitekop.com |access-date=6 August 2013}}</ref> managed by former Rangers player-manager [[Graeme Souness]].<ref>{{cite news|last=Davies |first=Christopher |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/teams/blackburn-rovers/3035810/Souness-prepares-for-Scottish-reunion.html |title=Souness prepares for Scottish reunion |work=The Telegraph |date=9 October 2002 |access-date=6 August 2013}}</ref> Celtic played poorly in the first leg at Parkhead, but a Henrik Larsson goal six minutes from full-time secured a crucial 1–0 win on the night and a narrow lead to take down to [[Ewood Park]].<ref>{{Cite news|date=2002-10-31|title=Larsson stuns Blackburn|language=en-GB|work=[[BBC Sport]]|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/uefa_cup/2369811.stm|access-date=2021-07-26}}</ref> In the build-up to the return match, Blackburn captain [[Gary Flitcroft]] made public that Souness had commented in the dressing room after the first game that Blackburn were the better side and should have won: "That was men against boys."<ref>{{cite news|last=Forsyth |first=Roddy |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/teams/blackburn-rovers/3037870/Irish-argue-Celtics-case.html |title=Irish argue Celtic's case |work=The Telegraph |date=14 November 2002 |access-date=6 August 2013}}</ref> In the second leg, Celtic showed much more composure and scored after 14 minutes through Larsson. Celtic controlled the game after that and Chris Sutton scored another goal for Celtic in the second half, with Celtic winning 2–0 on the night and 3–0 on aggregate.<ref>{{cite news|first=Daniel|last=Taylor|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2002/nov/15/uefa.sport |title=Blackburn Rovers 0 - 2 Celtic (Celtic won 3-0 on aggregate)|work=The Guardian |date=2 December 2003|access-date=6 August 2013}}</ref> Celtic's third round UEFA cup opponents were [[Celta de Vigo]]. In a first-leg overshadowed by eccentric refereeing,<ref>{{cite news|last=McCarthy|first=David|title=Clueless Columbo; Nightmare referee is a mystery as raging O'Neill blows his top |url=http://www.thefreelibrary.com/Football%3A+CLUELESS+COLUMBO%3B+Nightmare+referee+is+a+mystery+as+raging...-a094717506|access-date=4 October 2013|newspaper=Daily Record|date=29 November 2002}}</ref> Henrik Larsson scored the only goal of the game in Glasgow.<ref>{{cite news|first=Graham|last=Clark |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2002/nov/29/uefa.sport |title=Celtic 1 - 0 Celta Vigo |work=The Guardian |date=28 November 2002|access-date=6 August 2013}}</ref> The return match in Spain was won 2–1 by Celta, levelling the aggregate score at 2–2, but [[John Hartson]]'s away goal won the tie for Celtic. This was the first time ever that Celtic had knocked out a Spanish club in European competition, and also the first time in 23 years that Celtic had remained in European competition beyond Christmas.<ref>{{cite news|first=Chris|last=Roberts at the Balaidos Stadium |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2002/dec/13/uefa.sport1 |title=Celta Vigo 2 - 1 Celtic (Celtic win on away goals) |work=The Guardian |date=12 December 2002|access-date=6 August 2013}}</ref>

[[File:Seville celtic fans.jpg|thumb|right|250px|Celtic fans at the UEFA Cup Final in Seville]]German [[Bundesliga]] team [[VfB Stuttgart]] were Celtic's fourth round opponents in the Spring of 2003. Celtic came from behind to win the first leg 3–1 at Parkhead,<ref>{{cite news|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/uefa_cup/2780267.stm |title=Petrov sinks Stuttgart |work=[[BBC Sport]] |date=21 February 2003 |access-date=6 August 2013}}</ref> while in the second leg, despite Celtic going up 2–0 early, a comeback from Stuttgart saw the Bundesliga side win 3–2 on the night, although Celtic would progress 5–4 on aggregate.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/europe/2798753.stm |title=Celtic kill off Stuttgart |work=[[BBC Sport]] |date=27 February 2003 |access-date=6 August 2013}}</ref> The quarter-finals saw another English [[Premier League]] opponent for Celtic, this time Liverpool. The first leg took place at Celtic Park on 13 March 2003, ending 1–1 through a first-minute Larsson goal; the away goal, however, gave Liverpool an advantage entering their home tie at Anfield.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/europe/2836319.stm |title=Liverpool hold Celtic |work=[[BBC Sport]] |date=13 March 2003 |access-date=6 August 2013}}</ref> In the first leg, Liverpool player [[El Hadji Diouf]] spat at a Celtic supporter, and was later fined £5,000 at Glasgow's [[Sheriff Court]] for the incident.<ref>{{cite news |title=Diouf will not face assault charges |work=[[Raidió Teilifís Éireann]]|url=http://www.rte.ie/sport/soccer/english/2006/1116/211372-dioufe/ |access-date=25 January 2015 |date=14 June 2007}}</ref> The return match at Anfield took place the following week. Two minutes from half-time, Celtic were awarded a free-kick 25 yards from goal. [[Alan Thompson (footballer, born 1973)|Alan Thompson]] struck a low shot past [[Jerzy Dudek]] from the set piece, with the ball going under the defensive wall which had jumped up and appeared to distract the Liverpool goalkeeper. Celtic sealed a 2–0 victory on 82 minutes when John Hartson played a [[Push and run|one-two]] with Larsson, then drove a swerving shot from 25 yards out into the top-right corner of Dudek's goal.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/europe/2864921.stm |title=Celtic brush aside Liverpool |work=[[BBC Sport]] |date=20 March 2003 |access-date=6 August 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|last=Murray|first=Scott|title=Liverpool 0 - 2 Celtic (agg: 1-3)|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2003/mar/20/minutebyminute.sport|website=The Guardian|access-date=12 July 2015|date=20 March 2003}}</ref>

Celtic's first European semi-final since the early 1970s saw them paired against Portuguese side [[Boavista F.C.|Boavista]]. Celtic drew 1–1 in the first game at Parkhead,<ref>{{cite news|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/europe/2909819.stm |title=Celtic held by Boavista |work=[[BBC Sport]] |date=10 April 2003 |access-date=6 August 2013}}</ref> but a Henrik Larsson goal in the 80th minute of the return match in Portugal won the tie and ensured Celtic's progress to the UEFA Cup Final.<ref>{{cite news |first=Jon|last=Brodkin|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2003/apr/25/newsstory.sport4 |title=Boavista 0 - 1 Celtic (Agg: 1-2) |work=The Guardian |date=25 April 2003 |access-date=6 August 2013}}</ref>

Around 80,000 Celtic fans travelled to watch the club compete in the [[2003 UEFA Cup Final|UEFA Cup Final]], held in [[Seville]] on 21 May 2003.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.theguardian.com/observer/pictures/0,8561,972767,00.html |title=Celtic in Seville: A picture special |work=Observer Sport (The Guardian) |access-date=25 January 2015}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|last1=Stewart|first1=Stephen|last2=Henderson|first2=Damien|title=Hats off to very good bhoys and ghirls 80,000 Celtic fans in Spain and not a single arrest|url=http://www.heraldscotland.com/sport/spl/aberdeen/hats-off-to-very-good-bhoys-and-ghirls-80-000-celtic-fans-in-spain-and-not-a-single-arrest-1.117903|work=Herald Scotland|access-date=25 January 2015|date=23 May 2003}}</ref> Played in humid weather, the first half was a somewhat stoic affair, but Porto scored just before half-time when [[Derlei]] netted [[Dmitri Alenichev]]'s parried shot. Celtic levelled two minutes into the second half when Henrik Larsson headed Didier Agathe's cross from the right past goalkeeper Vitor Baiai. Porto regained the lead five minutes later when [[Deco]]'s angled pass to Alenichev saw the Russian score. Again Celtic rallied, with Larsson scoring with another header, this time from an Alan Thompson corner. Celtic were however frustrated by the referee's over-indulgence of Porto's time-wasting and their players' persistent feigning of injury. With the game tied at 2–2 on 90 minutes, the final went into extra time. [[Bobo Baldé]] was sent off on 95 minutes after his second yellow card. On 112 minutes Derlei reacted quickest to a Rab Douglas block and rounded Jackie McNamara to make it 3–2. Porto managed to hang on to win the UEFA Cup.<ref name="rte">{{cite news|url=http://www.rte.ie/sport/soccer/european/2003/0522/172812-celtic01/ |title=UEFA may discipline O'Neill over comments |work=RTÉ Sport |date=14 June 2007 |access-date=6 August 2013}}</ref><ref name="fin1">{{cite web|title=Derlei decides dramatic final|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/match/71041--celtic-vs-porto/|website=[[UEFA]]|access-date=3 January 2016|date=22 May 2003}}</ref> Nonetheless, the exemplary conduct of the thousands of travelling Celtic supporters received widespread praise from the people of Seville and the fans were awarded prestigious [[FIFA Fair Play Award|Fair Play Awards]] from both [[FIFA]] and UEFA "for their extraordinarily loyal and sporting behaviour".<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.fifa.com/ballondor/archive/edition=1999902003/news/newsid=90249/index.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121223015512/http://www.fifa.com/ballondor/archive/edition=1999902003/news/newsid=90249/index.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=23 December 2012 |title=Celebrating Celtic pride in the heart of Andalusia |website=FIFA.com |date=15 December 2003 |access-date=6 August 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/teams/c/celtic/3189853.stm |title=Celtic fans 'Europe's best' |work=[[BBC Sport]] |date=28 August 2003 |access-date=6 August 2013}}</ref>

Celtic qualified for the Champions League in [[2003–04 UEFA Champions League|2003–04]], but again could only finish third in the group stages and therefore dropped into the [[2003–04 UEFA Cup|UEFA Cup]].<ref>{{cite news|title=Celtic suffer Lyon heartbreak; Lyon 3-2 Celtic|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/champions_league/3304353.stm|access-date=3 March 2014|date=10 December 2003|work=[[BBC Sport]]}}</ref> After a straightforward aggregate win over [[FK Teplice]],<ref>{{cite web|title=FK Teplice 1-0 Celtic: Safe passage |url=http://espnfc.com/en/report/142294/report.html?soccernet=true |work=ESPN FC |access-date=4 March 2014 |date=3 March 2004 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140304221913/http://espnfc.com/en/report/142294/report.html?soccernet=true |archive-date= 4 March 2014 }}</ref> Celtic were drawn against [[FC Barcelona|Barcelona]] in the round of 16. A dramatic first leg in Glasgow saw Celtic win 1–0 through an Alan Thompson goal, but goalkeeper Rab Douglas and Barcelona's [[Thiago Motta]] were sent off during half-time for fighting in the tunnel.<ref>{{cite web|title=Celtic spoil Barça's winning streak|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/019b-0e6c0f2dad5c-c0ecb069152e-1000--celtic-spoil-barca-s-winning-streak/|website=[[UEFA]]|access-date=3 March 2014|date=11 March 2004}}</ref> The return match in Spain saw Celtic fielding 19-year-old reserve goalkeeper [[David Marshall (Scottish footballer)|David Marshall]] in place of Douglas and young defender [[John Kennedy (Scottish footballer)|John Kennedy]] standing in for Bobo Baldé, who was also suspended.<ref name="0416r">{{cite news|title=Celtic stun Barcelona; Barcelona 0-0 Celtic (agg:0-1)|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/teams/c/celtic/3560199.stm|work=[[BBC Sport]]|access-date=3 March 2014|date=25 March 2004}}</ref> Celtic found themselves under a sustained barrage of pressure, but the two youngsters turned in outstanding performances. Marshall pulled off outstanding saves from [[Luis Enrique (footballer)|Luis Enrique]] and [[Luis García (footballer, born 1978)|Luis García]], while Kennedy made a number of great tackles to keep Barcelona at bay.<ref name="0416r" /> Celtic held out for a scoreless draw on the night to win on aggregate and progress to the quarter-finals.<ref name="0416r" /> Celtic's run in Europe was finally ended in the next round by another Spanish side, [[Villarreal CF|Villarreal]], losing 3–1 on aggregate.<ref>{{cite news|title=Villarreal 2-0 Celtic (Villarreal win 3-1 on aggregate)|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/europe/3621937.stm|work=[[BBC Sport]]|access-date=4 March 2014|date=14 April 2004}}</ref> The club went straight into the group stages for the [[2004–05 UEFA Champions League]], but finished bottom of their group on five points, one point behind [[FC Shakhtar Donetsk|Shakhtar Donetsk]] who qualified for the UEFA Cup spot, and behind Barcelona and [[AC Milan]] who progressed to the next stage of the Champions League.<ref>{{cite news|title=O'Neill proud of Celtic efforts|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/europe/4073407.stm|access-date=8 December 2016|work=[[BBC Sport]]|date=7 December 2004}}</ref> This was Martin O'Neill's last European campaign with Celtic, with him resigning in May 2005 to care for his ill wife.<ref>{{cite news|last=Lamont |first=Alasdair |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/teams/c/celtic/4576981.stm |title=End of an era as O'Neill resigns |work=[[BBC Sport]] |date=25 May 2005 |access-date=6 August 2013}}</ref>

[[Gordon Strachan]] made an unpromising start as manager of Celtic in the summer of 2005, losing his first match in charge – a [[2005–06 UEFA Champions League|Champions League]] qualifier – 0–5 to Slovak side [[FC Petržalka akadémia|Artmedia Bratislava]], the club's worst ever European result at that time.<ref>{{cite news |work=[[BBC Sport]] |title=Artmedia 5-0 Celtic |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/teams/c/celtic/4718461.stm |date=27 July 2005 |access-date=14 September 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|date=13 July 2016|title=Celtic's worst defeats in Europe|work=The Herald|url=https://www.heraldscotland.com/sport/14615718.celtics-worst-defeats-in-europe/|access-date=14 September 2020}}</ref> Celtic did produce a fight-back in the second leg at Parkhead, but their 4–0 win on the night was not enough to salvage the tie.<ref name="cie" />

[[File:Celtic Benfica CL0708.jpg|thumb|left|300px|Celtic Park before kick-off in a Champions League tie against Benfica in November 2007]]
The following season though saw Strachan guide Celtic into last 16 of the [[2006–07 UEFA Champions League|Champions League]] for the first time. Celtic secured their progress from the group stages courtesy of [[Shunsuke Nakamura]]'s 30-yard free-kick against [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]] in a 1–0 win at Parkhead. The win on the night, and qualification to the last 16, was clinched when [[Artur Boruc]] saved [[Louis Saha]]'s 88th-minute penalty kick.<ref>{{cite web|last=McCarra|first=Kevin|date=22 November 2006|title=United crash as Nakamura puts Celtic through|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2006/nov/22/match.sport|access-date=25 January 2015|work=[[The Guardian]]}}</ref> Celtic lost 0–1 in the last 16 round to the eventual winners of the cup, [[AC Milan]], after a goal from [[Kaká]] in extra time.<ref>{{cite web|last=Aikman|first=Richard|title=Celtic foiled by Kaká magic |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/01bf-0e6e341eba25-230916a3413f-1000--celtic-foiled-by-kaka-magic/|website=[[UEFA]]|date=8 March 2007|access-date=8 October 2013}}</ref>

Strachan took Celtic to the last 16 of the Champions League again in [[2007–08 UEFA Champions League|2007–08]], were they paired up against Barcelona. The first leg was played in Glasgow, and Celtic twice took the lead through goals from [[Jan Vennegoor of Hesselink]] and [[Barry Robson]]. Barcelona fought back, however, to win 3–2 on the night. In the return match, a goal by [[Xavi (footballer, born 1980)|Xavi]] saw Barça win 1–0 in Spain and knock Celtic out 4–2 on aggregate.<ref name="cie" />

[[Tony Mowbray]] took charge of Celtic in 2009. The club's final league position of the previous season meant that two qualifying rounds were required to enter the Champions League group stages. After a 1–0 loss to [[FC Dynamo Moscow|Dynamo Moscow]] at home,<ref>{{cite news|last=Grahame|first=Ewing|title=Celtic 0 Dinamo Moscow 1: match report|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/teams/celtic/5934557/Celtic-0-Dinamo-Moscow-1-match-report.html|work=The Telegraph|access-date=30 October 2013|date=29 July 2009}}</ref> Mowbray lead Celtic to a 2–0 win in [[Moscow]], [[Georgios Samaras]]' injury time goal clinching the tie on aggregate and ending a long run of not winning any away games in the Champions League.<ref>{{Cite news|last=McDaid|first=David|date=2009-08-05|title=D'mo Moscow 0-2 Celtic (agg 1-2)|language=en-GB|work=[[BBC Sport]]|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/europe/8178985.stm|access-date=2021-07-26}}</ref> This was also the first time ever that Celtic had won a European tie after losing the first leg at home.<ref>{{cite news |title=Dinamo Moscow 0 Celtic 2: match report |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/teams/celtic/5979246/Dinamo-Moscow-0-Celtic-2-match-report.html |first=Ronnie |last=Esplin |work=The Telegraph |access-date=14 September 2020 |date=5 August 2009}}</ref> Celtic were then drawn with English side [[Arsenal F.C.|Arsenal]],<ref>{{cite news|title=Champions League draw: Arsenal to face Celtic in play-off|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/teams/arsenal/5988106/Champions-League-draw-Arsenal-to-face-Celtic-in-play-off.html|work=The Telegraph|access-date=31 October 2013|date=7 August 2009}}</ref> where they would lose the home leg 2–0.<ref>{{Cite news|last=Moffat|first=Colin|date=2009-08-18|title=Celtic 0-2 Arsenal|language=en-GB|work=[[BBC Sport]]|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/europe/8204043.stm|access-date=2021-07-26}}</ref> In the return leg in the [[Emirates Stadium]], Celtic were holding Arsenal 0–0 until Arsenal striker [[Eduardo da Silva|Eduardo]] won his club a controversial penalty, which he himself converted. Arsenal went on to win 3–1 win on the night, with [[Massimo Donati]] scoring Celtic's only goal in the last minute.<ref>{{cite news|last=Winter|first=Henry|title=Arsenal 3 Celtic 1; agg 5-1: match report|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/competitions/champions-league/6095206/Arsenal-3-Celtic-1-agg-5-1-match-report.html|work=The Telegraph|access-date=31 October 2013|date=26 August 2009}}</ref> Things began to slide after that, however, and Celtic exited the rebranded [[UEFA Europa League]] before Christmas despite a promising draw of group opponents.<ref>{{Cite news|last=Lindsay|first=Clive|date=2009-12-02|title=Celtic 2-0 Hapoel Tel-Aviv|language=en-GB|work=[[BBC Sport]]|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/europe/8389229.stm|access-date=2021-07-26}}</ref>

===2010–2020===
====Neil Lennon & Ronny Deila====
[[Neil Lennon]] became manager in 2010, but despite winning their home games, Celtic went out of both the [[2010–11 UEFA Champions League|Champions League]] and [[2010–11 UEFA Europa League|Europa League]] at the first hurdles, losing 4–2 on aggregate against both eventual [[2011 UEFA Europa League Final|Europa League finalists]] [[S.C. Braga|Braga]] and Dutch side [[FC Utrecht|Utrecht]].<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.irishtimes.com/sports/soccer/2010/0826/1224277683720.html|title=Dutch disaster for Celtic|newspaper=The Irish Times|date=26 August 2010 |access-date=18 July 2011}}</ref>

Celtic qualified for the [[2011–12 UEFA Europa League|Europa League]] [[2011–12 UEFA Europa League group stage|group stages]] in 2011–12 in bizarre circumstances. Swiss side [[FC Sion|Sion]] defeated Celtic 3–1 on aggregate in the qualifying tie.<ref>{{cite web|date=25 August 2011|title=BBC Sport - Sion 3-1 Celtic (agg 3-1)|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/14569496|access-date=12 November 2013|work=[[BBC Sport]]}}</ref> Sion, however, were thrown out of the tournament by UEFA due to them fielding ineligible players in the two games against Celtic.<ref name="fcs">{{cite web|first=Ewan |last=Murray |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2011/sep/02/celtic-europa-league-fc-sion |title=Celtic back in Europa League after successful protest against FC Sion |work=The Guardian |date=2 September 2011 |access-date=12 November 2013}}</ref> Celtic were thus reinstated, eventually finishing third in Group I, behind Atlético Madrid and [[Udinese Calcio|Udinese]] and ahead of [[Stade Rennais F.C.|Rennes]].<ref>{{cite news|last=Campbell |first=Andy |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/teams/c/celtic/9662325.stm |title=Udinese v Celtic as it happened |work=[[BBC Sport]] |date=15 December 2011 |access-date=12 November 2013}}</ref>

As Scottish Champions from 2011 to 2012, Celtic had the opportunity to take part in the [[2012–13 UEFA Champions League|2012–13 Champions League]]. However, two qualifying rounds had first to be negotiated, which Celtic managed to do with 4–1 and 4–0 aggregate wins over HJK Helsinki and [[Helsingborgs IF]]. Celtic, as fourth-ranked seeds, found themselves drawn in [[2012–13 UEFA Champions League group stage#Group G|Group G]] with Barcelona, Benfica and [[FC Spartak Moscow|Spartak Moscow]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/19424943 |title=Champions League: Celtic to face Barcelona, Benfica & Spartak |work=[[BBC Sport]] |date=30 August 2012 |access-date=12 November 2013}}</ref>

On 2 October 2012, Celtic achieved their first-ever away win in the group stages of the Champions League with a 3–2 win in Russia over Spartak Moscow, [[Georgios Samaras]] scoring the winning goal in the 90th minute.<ref>{{Cite news|last=Campbell|first=Andy|date=2 October 2012|title=Spartak Moscow 2-3 Celtic|language=en-GB|work=[[BBC Sport]]|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/19709684|access-date=2021-07-26}}</ref> Celtic's home match with Barcelona in November 2012 coincided with the week of Celtic's 125th Anniversary.<ref>{{Cite news|date=7 November 2012|title=Celtic 2-1 Barcelona: Listen to best bits|language=en-GB|work=[[BBC Sport]]|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/av/football/20246561|access-date=2021-07-26}}</ref> As such, an "[[Ultras]]"-styled section of the Celtic support called the [[Green Brigade]] organised a full stadium pre-match card display (a "[[tifo]]") to celebrate the club's 125th anniversary.<ref>{{cite news|title=Incredible pre-match display from supporters |url=http://www.celticfc.net/newsstory?item=3319 |website=Celtic F.C. |date=7 November 2012 |access-date=8 November 2012 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131112134748/http://www.celticfc.net/newsstory?item=3319 |archive-date=12 November 2013 }}</ref> The display featured a [[Celtic cross]], green and white hoops and "125 Celtic" in written form, with supporters earning the praise of club chairman [[Peter Lawwell]].<ref>{{cite news|title=Peter Lawwell congratulates team and support on collective effort |url=http://www.celticfc.net/newsstory?item=3325 |website=Celtic F.C. |date=8 November 2012 |access-date=8 November 2012 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131112134645/http://www.celticfc.net/newsstory?item=3325 |archive-date=12 November 2013 }}</ref> A memorable night was completed when goals from [[Victor Wanyama]] and 18-year-old striker [[Tony Watt]] gave Celtic [[Celtic F.C. 2–1 FC Barcelona|a shock 2–1 win over Barcelona]].<ref>{{cite web|last=Lamont |first=Alasdair |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/20150451 |title=Celtic 2-1 Barcelona |work=[[BBC Sport]] |date=7 November 2012 |access-date=12 November 2013}}</ref> Goalkeeper [[Fraser Forster]] produced an outstanding performance in the game, winning the praise of the Spanish media who nicknamed him "''La Gran Muralla''" ("The Great Wall").<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.huffingtonpost.co.uk/2012/11/08/fraser-forster-great-wall_n_2091816.html |title=Fraser Forster Great Wall |work=HuffPost|date=8 November 2012 |access-date=8 November 2012 }}</ref>

Celtic secured their progress to the knock-out stages of the Champions League on 5 December 2012 with a 2–1 home win over Spartak Moscow, [[Kris Commons]] scoring the winning goal in 82 minutes with a penalty.<ref>{{cite web|last=Lamont |first=Alasdair |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/20522174 |title=Celtic 2-1 Spartak Moscow |work=[[BBC Sport]] |date=5 December 2012 |access-date=12 November 2013}}</ref> Celtic were drawn against Juventus but succumbed to 5–0 aggregate defeat in the Spring of 2013 to go out of the tournament. Despite the emphatic score line over the two legs, Celtic were not outclassed by their Italian opponents in terms of general play but while Juventus defended with a ruthless defiance, Celtic in contrast shipped their goals all far too easily.<ref>{{Cite news|last=Crawford|first=Kenny|date=6 March 2013|title=Juventus 2-0 Celtic (agg 5-0)|language=en-GB|work=[[BBC Sport]]|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/21598002|access-date=2021-07-26}}</ref> Despite the result against Juventus, Celtic and Neil Lennon won praise for their Champions League campaign.<ref>{{cite web|first=Tony |last=Haggerty |url=http://www.dailyrecord.co.uk/sport/football/football-news/celtic-boss-neil-lennon-just-2670834 |title=Celtic boss Neil Lennon is just like my old man Harry in the dugout, claims ex-England star Jamie Redknapp |work=Daily Record |date=3 November 2013 |access-date=12 November 2013 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131107032139/http://www.dailyrecord.co.uk/sport/football/football-news/celtic-boss-neil-lennon-just-2670834 |archive-date= 7 November 2013 }}</ref><ref>{{cite news|first=Jeff |last=Stelling |url=http://www.skysports.com/opinion/story/0,25212,12088_8235533,00.html |title=Celtic win over Barcelona compels Jeff Stelling to tackle a challenging problem |work=Sky Sports |date=8 November 2012 |access-date=12 November 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|first=Jeff |last=Stelling |url=http://www.skysports.com/opinion/story/0,25212,12088_8313877,00.html |title=Man City could learn a lot from Celtic's Champions League exploits |work=Sky Sports |date=6 December 2012 |access-date=12 November 2013}}</ref>

Celtic began the [[2013–14 UEFA Champions League|2013–14 season]] with further Champions League success in their sights. Celtic were drawn with [[Cliftonville F.C.|Cliftonville]] in their first qualifying round and easily disposed of the Northern Irish champions 5–0 on aggregate.<ref>{{cite web|title=Celtic 2 Cliftonville 0|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/23324257|work=[[BBC Sport]]|access-date=17 February 2014|date=23 July 2013}}</ref> They were next drawn with [[IF Elfsborg]] and squeezed by with a 1–0 aggregate win, with Kris Commons scoring the only goal in the first leg at Celtic Park.<ref>{{cite web|last=Campbell|first=Andy|title=Elfsborg 0 Celtic 0|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/23513157|work=[[BBC Sport]]|access-date=17 February 2014|date=7 August 2013}}</ref> Although favored to qualify for the group stages with minimal challenge after being drawn with Kazakh champions [[FC Shakhter Karagandy|Shakhter Karagandy]], Celtic found themselves losing the first leg in [[Karagandy]] 2–0.<ref>{{cite web|last=Grant|first=Michael|title=Shakhter Karagandy 2 Celtic 0: stirring cocktail leaves Lennon's side groggy|url=http://www.heraldscotland.com/sport/football/shakhter-karagandy-2-celtic-0-stirring-cocktail-leaves-lennon-s-side-groggy.1377061269|work=Herald Scotland|access-date=17 February 2014|date=21 August 2013}}</ref> Celtic returned to Scotland to an electric atmosphere at Celtic Park in the home leg, in what proved to be the high point of their 2013–14 Champions League campaign. They overcame the 2–0 deficit to level the aggregate thanks to goals by Kris Commons and Georgios Samaras. [[James Forrest (footballer, born 1991)|James Forrest]] completed the comeback for Celtic with a 90th-minute strike, sending Celtic Park into a frenzy.<ref>{{cite web|last=Lindsay|first=Clive|title=Celtic 3 Shakhter Karagandy 0|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/23776602|work=[[BBC Sport]]|access-date=17 February 2014|date=28 August 2013}}</ref>

Thereafter, Celtic found themselves drawn with AC Milan, Ajax and again Barcelona in the [[2013–14 UEFA Champions League group stage|group stage]].<ref>{{cite web|title=Barcelona, Milan, Ajax and Celtic drawn together |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0250-0c50fddbac4f-7d343c85c61d-1000--barcelona-milan-ajax-and-celtic-drawn-together/|website=[[UEFA]]|access-date=17 February 2014|date=29 August 2013}}</ref> The campaign was dismal, winning only once with a 2–1 home win over Ajax,<ref>{{cite web|last=Lamont|first=Alasdair|title=Celtic 2 Ajax 1|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/24614436|work=[[BBC Sport]]|access-date=17 February 2014|date=22 October 2013}}</ref> and slumping to a 6–1 rout away against Barcelona in the final group match.<ref name="thrash">{{cite web|last=Lamont|first=Alasdair|title=Barcelona 6 Celtic 1 |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/25303700|work=[[BBC Sport]]|access-date=17 February 2014|date=11 December 2013}}</ref> That game saw Celtic concede the most goals they had ever done in a single European tie, and equaled their previous heaviest defeat in Europe, a 5–0 loss to Artmedia Bratislava.<ref name="thrash" />

[[Ronny Deila]]'s first competitive match in charge of Celtic was a [[2014–15 UEFA Champions League qualifying phase and play-off round|Champions League qualifying]] tie away against [[Knattspyrnufélag Reykjavíkur|KR Reykjavik]] on 15 July 2014, which ended in a 1–0 win with midfielder [[Callum McGregor]] scoring the decisive goal on 84 minutes.<ref>{{cite web|last=McVake|first=Roddie|title=KR Reykjavík 0 - 1 Celtic |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/28278531|work=[[BBC Sport]]|access-date=27 January 2015|date=15 July 2014}}</ref> Celtic eased to a 4–0 win in the return match in Scotland,<ref>{{cite web|title=Celtic 4-0 KR Reykjavik (agg 5-0)|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/28331345|work=[[BBC Sport]]|access-date=6 August 2014|date=22 July 2014}}</ref> but were comprehensively beaten and outplayed in the next qualifying round, losing 6–1 on aggregate to [[Legia Warsaw]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/28656118 |access-date=1 August 2014 |title=Celtic 0 - 2 Legia Warsaw |date=6 August 2014 |first=Alasdair |last=Lamont}}</ref> Despite this, Celtic were given a reprieve when it was discovered that Legia had fielded an ineligible player in the second leg, [[Bartosz Bereszynski]] coming on as a substitute in the last four minutes. UEFA punished the Polish club by awarding the game a 3–0 to Celtic, levelling the aggregate score at 4–4 and seeing the Scottish champions progress on away goals.<ref name="legia">{{cite web|title=Celtic reinstated to Champions League after Uefa punishes Legia Warsaw|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/aug/08/celtic-reinstated-champions-league-uefa-legia-warsaw|work=The Guardian|access-date=8 August 2014|date=8 August 2014}}</ref> Despite this astonishing piece of luck, Celtic failed abjectly to take advantage in the final qualifier against [[NK Maribor|Maribor]], losing 2–1 on aggregate to drop down to the [[2014–15 UEFA Europa League|Europa League]].<ref>{{cite web|title=Celtic 0 - 1 NK Maribor|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/28927666|work=[[BBC Sport]]|access-date=26 August 2014|date=26 August 2014}}</ref>

Celtic qualified from their [[2014–15 UEFA Europa League group stage#Group D|Europa League group]].<ref>{{cite web|last=Wilson|first=Richard|title=Celtic 1 - 3 FC Red Bull Salzburg|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/30204414|work=[[BBC Sport]]|access-date=7 February 2015|date=27 November 2014}}</ref> They played Inter Milan in the [[2014–15 UEFA Europa League knockout phase|last 16 stage]] of the Europa League, rallying to draw 3–3 at Parkhead from an early 0–2 deficit in a pulsating encounter,<ref>{{cite web|last=Fosyth|first=Roddy|title=Celtic 3 Inter Milan 3, match report: John Guidetti scores last-gasp equaliser to keep hosts alive in tie|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/teams/celtic/11423375/Celtic-3-Inter-Milan-3-match-report-John-Guidetti-scores-last-gasp-equaliser-to-keep-hosts-alive-in-tie.html|work=The Telegraph|access-date=3 March 2015|date=19 February 2015}}</ref> then losing 1–0 in Milan to go out on aggregate despite a highly credible performance.<ref>{{cite web|last=Mullen|first=Scott|title=Inter 1 Celtic 0 (agg: 4-3): Hoops exit Europe after Guarin's wonder strike|url=http://www.eveningtimes.co.uk/celtic/inter-1-celtic-0-agg-4-3-hoops-exit-europe-after-guarins-wonder-strike-198656n.119510154|work=Evening Times|access-date=3 March 2015|date=26 February 2015}}</ref>

Celtic began the [[2015–16 UEFA Champions League qualifying phase and play-off round|2015–16 season]] again attempting to qualify for the Champions League. They knocked out [[Stjarnan men's football|Stjarnan]] from Iceland<ref>{{cite web|last=Lindsay|first=Clive|title=Stjarnan 1 - 4 Celtic|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/33609346|access-date=30 August 2015|date=22 July 2015 |work=[[BBC Sport]]}}</ref> and [[Qarabağ FK|Qarabağ]] from Azerbaijan in the early qualifying rounds,<ref>{{cite web|last=Campbell|first=Andy|title=FK Qarabag 0 - 0 Celtic|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/33755230|website=[[BBC Sport]]|access-date=30 August 2015|date=5 August 2015}}</ref> but lost 4–3 on aggregate to Swedish side [[Malmö FF]] in the final play-off round. This meant for the second successive season, Celtic dropped into the Europa League.<ref>{{cite web|last=Campbell|first=Andy|title=Malmo 2 - 0 Celtic|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/34047527|website=[[BBC Sport]]|access-date=30 August 2015|date=25 August 2015}}</ref> Celtic's [[2015–16 UEFA Europa League|Europa League campaign]] was poor and littered with individual errors which contributed to dropped points in every match.<ref name="el1516">{{cite news|last=Lamont|first=Alasdair|title=Fenerbahçe 1 - 1 Celtic|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/35020011|access-date=12 February 2016|work=[[BBC Sport]]|date=10 December 2015}}</ref> Celtic failed to qualify after finishing bottom of [[2015–16 UEFA Europa League group stage#Group A|their group]], and was the first time Celtic went through a group stage of a European competition without recording a single victory.<ref name="el1516" />

====Brendan Rodgers====
Celtic's first competitive match under new manager [[Brendan Rodgers]], saw the team [[Lincoln Red Imps 1–0 Celtic|lose 1–0]] against [[Gibraltar]]ian minnows [[Lincoln Red Imps F.C.|Lincoln Red Imps]] in the first leg of the Second Round [[2016–17 UEFA Champions League#Qualifying rounds|2016–17 Champions League]] qualifiers, a result described by ''The Scotsman'' as a "humiliation".<ref>{{cite news|title=Lincoln Red Imps 1 - 0 Celtic: Stunning upset in Rodgers' first match|url=http://www.scotsman.com/sport/football/teams/celtic/lincoln-red-imps-1-0-celtic-stunning-upset-in-rodgers-first-match-1-4175957|access-date=30 July 2016|work=The Scotsman|date=12 July 2016}}</ref> Celtic won the second leg 3–0 to progress on aggregate and went on to eliminate [[FC Astana]] and [[Hapoel Be'er Sheva F.C|Hapoel Be'er Sheva]] in the subsequent qualifying rounds to progress into the Champions League group stages for the first time since 2013.<ref>{{cite news|last=English|first=Tom|title=Celtic 2 - 1 FC Astana 1|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/36955619|access-date=13 September 2016|work=[[BBC Sport]]|date=3 August 2016}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last=Esplin|first=Ronnie|title=Celtic vs Hapoel Be'er Sheva: Brendan Rodgers praises major shareholder after surviving Champions League scare|url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/european/celtic-vs-hapoel-beer-sheva-brendan-rodgers-praises-major-shareholder-after-surviving-champions-a7206711.html|access-date=13 September 2016|work=The Independent|date=24 August 2016}}</ref> The team's opening match in the Champions League proper on 13 September 2016 proved to be a humbling experience though as [[FC Barcelona|Barcelona]] delivered a footballing lesson in a 7–0 rout; the club's heaviest ever European defeat and the second heaviest in the club's history.<ref name="gubbed70">{{cite news|last=English|first=Tom|title=Barcelona 7 - 0 Celtic|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/37331224|access-date=14 September 2016|work=[[BBC Sport]]|date=13 September 2016}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=Alltime club records|url=http://www.fitbastats.com/celtic/club_records_overall.php|website=FitbaStats|access-date=14 September 2016}}</ref> A level of pride was restored in the next Champions League tie, Celtic holding [[Manchester City F.C.|Manchester City]] to a 3–3 draw at Celtic Park, with new signing [[Moussa Dembélé (French footballer)|Moussa Dembélé]] scoring twice, and ending the high-spending English club's run of 10 consecutive wins.<ref>{{cite news|last=Taylor|first=Daniel|title=Celtic end Manchester City's winning run with thrilling Champions League draw|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2016/sep/28/celtic-manchester-city-champions-league-match-report|access-date=19 November 2016|work=The Guardian|date=28 September 2016}}</ref> Further draws away at [[Borussia Mönchengladbach]] and in the return match at Manchester City saw Celtic finish the group on three points, coming fourth and going out of Europe.<ref>{{cite news|title=Celtic: Brendan Rodgers says Champions League campaign 'sets standard'|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/38228840|access-date=13 February 2017|work=[[BBC Sport]]|date=6 December 2016}}</ref>

Celtic entered the [[2017–18 UEFA Champions League|2017–18 Champions League]] at the 2nd qualifying round; they defeated [[Linfield F.C.|Linfield]] 6–0, [[Rosenborg BK|Rosenborg]] 1–0 and [[FC Astana|Astana]] 8–4 on aggregate to enter the group stages.<ref>{{cite news|title=Celtic 4 Linfield 0|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/39330673|access-date=30 April 2018|work=[[BBC Sport]]|date=19 July 2017}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=Rosenborg 0 Celtic 1|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/40732241|access-date=30 April 2018|work=[[BBC Sport]]|date=2 August 2017}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=Astana 4 Celtic 3|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/40997756|access-date=30 April 2018|work=[[BBC Sport]]|date=22 August 2017}}</ref> Celtic gained only three points over the six games with a 3–0 away win – their first European group stage victory in 16 attempts – at [[R.S.C. Anderlecht|Anderlecht]],<ref>{{cite news|title=RSC Anderlecht 0 Celtic 3|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/41372730|access-date=30 April 2018|work=[[BBC Sport]]|date=22 September 2017}}</ref> and finished in third place despite losing 5–0 and 7–1 to [[Paris Saint-Germain F.C.|Paris Saint Germain]].<ref>{{cite news|title=Paris St-Germain 7 Celtic 1|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/42056051|access-date=30 April 2018|work=[[BBC Sport]]|date=22 November 2017}}</ref> By finishing third in the Champions League group, Celtic qualified for the knockout Round of 32 in the [[2017–18 UEFA Europa League|Europa League]] where they were drawn against [[FC Zenit Saint Petersburg|Zenit Saint Petersburg]]. Celtic won the first leg 1–0 after a late winner by [[Callum McGregor]], but succumbed to a disappointing 3–0 defeat in [[Saint Petersburg]] and were subsequently knocked out of the competition.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/43136856|title=Zenit St Petersburg 3 Celtic 0|date=22 February 2018|work=[[BBC Sport]]|access-date=30 April 2018}}</ref>

Celtic were eliminated from the [[2018–19 UEFA Champions League qualifying phase and play-off round|2018–19 Champions League qualifying phase]] by [[AEK Athens F.C.|AEK Athens]] in the penultimate round,<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/football/2018/08/14/celtic-crash-champions-league-qualifying-second-leg-defeat-aek/|title=Celtic crash out of Champions League qualifying with second-leg defeat to AEK Athens|work=The Daily Telegraph|first=Roddy |last=Forsyth|date=14 August 2018|access-date=21 September 2018}}</ref> subsequently progressing to the group stage of the [[2018–19 UEFA Europa League|Europa League]], where they were drawn alongside the two clubs with strong links to the [[Red Bull]] company<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/european/rb-leipzig-fc-salzburg-champions-league-clear-red-bull-link-a7799726.html|title=German clubs won't appeal after Uefa clear RB Leipzig and FC Salzburg for Champions League despite Red Bull link|work=[[The Independent]]|date=20 June 2017 |access-date=21 September 2018}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.dw.com/en/europa-league-draw-leipzig-drawn-with-sister-club-salzburg-frankfurt-have-it-tough/a-45305086|title=Europa League Draw: Leipzig drawn with 'sister' club Salzburg, Frankfurt have it tough|work=[[Deutsche Welle]]|date=31 August 2018|access-date=21 September 2018}}</ref> – [[RB Leipzig]] and [[FC Red Bull Salzburg|Red Bull Salzburg]] – along with Rosenborg.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.skysports.com/football/rosenborg-vs-celtic/399196|title=Rosenborg 0-0 Celtic (Agg: 1-3): Brendan Rodgers' side advance|work=Sky Sports|date=2 August 2018|access-date=21 September 2018}}</ref> In the [[2018–19 UEFA Europa League group stage#Group B|opening round of fixtures]], Celtic secured a narrow 1–0 home victory over the Norwegian champions through a header by [[Leigh Griffiths]] in the closing minutes;<ref name=rborgbbc>{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/45554016|title=Celtic 1–0 Rosenborg |author=Tom English|date=20 September 2018|work=[[BBC Sport]]|access-date=21 September 2018}}</ref><ref name=rborgguard>{{cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2018/sep/20/celtic-rosenborg-europa-league-match-report|title=Leigh Griffiths lifts Celtic's frustration with late winner against Rosenborg|date=20 September 2018|work=The Guardian|access-date=21 September 2018}}</ref> in doing so they achieved a win on Matchday 1 for the first time in fifteen group stage participations across the two UEFA competitions (dating back to 2001),<ref name=rborgguard/> as well as a first group stage win at Celtic Park in eleven attempts (since Dinamo Zagreb were beaten in 2014).<ref name=rborgbbc/> Celtic exchanged wins with RB Leipzig, and Red Bull Salzburg defeated their '[[Red Bull#Sports sponsorships and acquisitions|sister team]]' Leipzig and Celtic in both home and away ties.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.dw.com/en/europa-league-rb-leipzig-hanging-by-a-thread-after-salzburg-loss/a-46514308|title=Europa League: RB Leipzig hanging by a thread after Salzburg loss|work=[[Deutsche Welle]] |date=29 November 2018|access-date=19 December 2018}}</ref> Rosenborg helped Celtic to qualify from the group despite the Scottish champions losing at home to Salzburg; the Norwegians scoring a late equalizer against Leipzig in Germany which put the ''Hoops'' ahead on points when they had been behind the German side on the tiebreak methods.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.balls.ie/football/watch-unlikely-rosenborg-goal-saved-celtics-blushes-402894|title=Watch: The Unlikely Rosenborg Goal That Saved Celtic's Blushes|website=Balls|date=14 December 2018|access-date=19 December 2018}}</ref> In the last 32 they were drawn to face [[Valencia CF|Valencia]], with the Spanish side subsequently winning the tie 3–0 on aggregate to knock Celtic out.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Lamont |first1=Alasdair |title=Valencia 1 – 0 Celtic (agg 3–0) |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/47252765 |access-date=21 August 2019 |work=[[BBC Sport]]|date=21 February 2019}}</ref>

====Return of Neil Lennon====
The early rounds of the [[2019–20 UEFA Champions League qualifying phase and play-off round|2019–20 Champions League qualifying phase]] saw Celtic ease past [[FK Sarajevo]] and [[Nõmme Kalju FC|Nõmme Kalju]] with comfortable aggregate victories.<ref>{{cite news |last=Dowden |first=Martin |title=Celtic 2 - 1 FK Sarajevo |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/48969949 |access-date=30 November 2019 |work=[[BBC Sport]] |date=17 July 2019}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Lamont |first=Alasdair |title=Nõmme Kalju 0 - 2 Celtic |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/49158613 |access-date=30 November 2019 |work=[[BBC Sport]] |date=30 July 2019}}</ref> However, chaotic defending in the following round against Romanian champions [[CFR Cluj]] saw Celtic lose 4–3 at home (despite leading 2–1 and 3–2 at stages during the second half) to go out 5–4 on aggregate and drop down to the play-off round for the [[Europa League]].<ref>{{cite news |last=Lindsay |first=Matthew |title=Celtic 3 CFR Cluj 4 (aggregate 4-5): Neil Lennon's side crash out of Champions League |date=13 August 2019 |url=https://www.heraldscotland.com/sport/17834862.celtic-3-cluj-4-five-talking-points-celtic-crash-champions-league/ |access-date=30 November 2019 |work=Herald Scotland}}</ref> [[Billel Omrani]] was particularly influential for the Romanians, scoring twice and providing an assist for their opening goal.<ref>{{cite news |last=English |first=Tom |title=Celtic 3 - 4 Cluj |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/49323840 |access-date=30 November 2019 |work=[[BBC Sport]] |date=13 August 2019}}</ref> European football until December was guaranteed, however, with a 6–1 aggregate win over [[Allmänna Idrottsklubben|AIK Stockholm]] to progress to the group stages of the Europa League.<ref>{{cite news |last=Mullen |first=Scott |title=AIK 1 - 4 Celtic |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/49498377 |access-date=30 November 2019 |work=[[BBC Sport]] |date=29 August 2019}}</ref>

Celtic were drawn to face CFR Cluj again in the group stage, as well as [[Coppa Italia|Italian]] and [[Coupe de France|French cup]] winners [[S.S. Lazio|Lazio]] and [[Stade Rennais F.C.|Rennes]]. In the opening fixture in France, Celtic and Rennes exchanged penalties for a 1–1 draw, the same score line as when the pair first played each other eight years before.<ref>{{cite news |last=Idessane |first=Kheredine |title=Rennes 1 - 1 Celtic |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/49663645 |access-date=9 February 2020 |work=[[BBC Sport]] |date=19 September 2019}}</ref>
Celtic avenged their Champions League exit to CFR Cluj by convincingly beating the Romanians 2–0 in Glasgow, before a double header against title-chasing Lazio.<ref>{{cite news |last=Idessane |first=Kheredine |title=Celtic 2 - 0 Cluj |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/49832215 |access-date=9 February 2020 |work=[[BBC Sport]] |date=3 October 2019}}</ref> Celtic came from behind at home to beat the Italians 2–1, with an 89th-minute header from [[Christopher Jullien]] winning the game.<ref>{{cite news |last=Duncan |first=Thomas |title=Celtic 2 - 1 Lazio |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/50083556 |access-date=9 February 2020 |work=[[BBC Sport]] |date=24 October 2019}}</ref> Two weeks later Celtic headed to Rome, having never won on Italian soil before in 13 attempts: They sealed their place in the last 32 with another 2–1 victory over Lazio, again coming from behind.<ref>{{cite news |last=Idessane |first=Kheredine |title=Lazio 1 - 2 Celtic |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/50253786 |access-date=9 February 2020 |work=[[BBC Sport]] |date=7 November 2019}}</ref> This time it was a 95th-minute winner from [[Olivier Ntcham]] which sealed a famous double over the [[Serie A]] side. Celtic went on to top a European group for the first time in their history with a home win over Rennes, avoiding defeat in their first five group games for the first time.<ref>{{cite news |last=English |first=Tom |title=Celtic 3 - 1 Rennes |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/50489724 |access-date=9 February 2020 |work=[[BBC Sport]] |date=28 November 2019}}</ref> Celtic played mostly fringe players in their final game in Romania, losing 2–0 in what was a "[[dead rubber]]" for the Scottish champions.<ref>{{cite news |last=Bienkowski |first=Stefan |title=Cluj 2 - 0 Celtic |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/50672297 |access-date=9 February 2020 |work=[[BBC Sport]] |date=12 December 2019}}</ref> In the last-32 round, Celtic went out 4–2 on aggregate to [[F.C. Copenhagen]], largely as a result of poor defending (described by ''BBC Sport'' as "calamitous") in the second leg at Celtic Park.<ref>{{cite news |last1=English |first1=Tom |title=Celtic 1 - 0 FC Copenhagen |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/51598722 |access-date=20 August 2021 |work=BBC Sport |date=27 February 2020}}</ref>

Due to the ongoing [[COVID-19 pandemic]], all ties in the qualifying rounds for the two European club competitions in season 2020–21, excluding the play-off round for the Champions League, were played as single leg matches, hosted by one of the teams decided by draw.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/025e-0fb6101cfdbf-8b878372d79d-1000--uefa-competitions-to-resume-in-august/ |title=UEFA competitions to resume in August |website=[[UEFA]]|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=17 June 2020 |access-date=17 June 2020}}</ref> Celtic were knocked out of the Champions League in the second qualifying round, losing 2–1 at home to [[Ferencvárosi TC|Ferencváros]],<ref>{{cite news |last=Lindsay |first=Clive |title=Celtic 2-1 Ferencvárosi TC |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/53884053 |access-date=7 December 2021 |work=BBC Sport |date=26 August 2020}}</ref> but successfully negotiated the other qualifying rounds to qualify for the group stages of the Europa League.<ref>{{cite news |last=Watt |first=Martin |title=FK Sarajevo 0-1 Celtic |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/54323830 |access-date=7 December 2021 |work=BBC Sport |date=1 October 2020}}</ref> Celtic thereafter had a poor campaign, losing four of the six group matches (including a 4–1 defeat against a [[Sparta Prague]] side having to field a virtual reserve team due to players being absent through COVID-19),<ref>{{cite news |title=Celtic humiliated by Sparta Prague as Julis hat-trick sends them bottom |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2020/nov/05/celtic-sparta-prague-europa-league-match-report |access-date=7 December 2021 |work=The Guardian |date=5 November 2020}}</ref> and finished bottom of the group.<ref>{{cite news |last=Lindsay |first=Clive |title=Celtic 3-2 Lille |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/55231022 |access-date=7 December 2021 |work=BBC Sport |date=10 December 2020}}</ref>

===2021–===
In the 2021/22 season Celtic were eliminated in the second qualifying round of the Champions League after a 2-3 defeat by [[FC Midtjylland|Midtjylland]].<ref>{{cite web |last1=Duncan |first1=Thomas |title=Midtjylland 2-1 Celtic (agg 3-2): Postecoglou's side undone in extra time |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/57972180 |website=bbc.co.uk |date=27 July 2021 |access-date=17 December 2025}}</ref> Celtic defeated [[FK Jablonec|Jablonec]] and [[AZ Alkmaar]] to reach the group stages of the Europa League.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Southwick |first1=Andrew |title=Celtic 3-0 Jablonec (agg 7-2) - Celtic cruise into Europa League play-off |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/58138071 |website=bbc.co.uk |date=11 August 2021 |access-date=17 December 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Watt |first1=Martin |title=AZ Alkmaar 2-1 Celtic: Scottish side progress 3-2 on aggregate |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/58306063 |website=bbc.co.uk |date=25 August 2021 |access-date=17 December 2025}}</ref> Celtic finished third in the group and qualified for the play-off round in the [[UEFA Europa Conference League]] where they lost 5-1 on aggregate to [[FK Bodø/Glimt|Bodø/Glimt]].<ref>{{cite web |last1=Morgan |first1=Richard |title=Bodo/Glimt 2-0 Celtic (Agg 5-1): Hoops exit Europa Conference League |url=https://www.skysports.com/football/news/11787/12548634/bodo-glimt-2-0-celtic-agg-5-1-hoops-exit-europa-conference-league |website=skysports.com |access-date=17 December 2025}}</ref>

In 2022, Celtic qualified for the [[2022–23_UEFA_Champions_League_group_stage|group stage]] of the [[2022–23 UEFA Champions League]] for the first time since the 2017/18 season. They were placed in Group F with [[FC Shakhtar Donetsk|Shakhtar Donetsk]], [[RB Leipzig]] and reigning champions [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]], ultimately finishing bottom of the group with two points.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Atkinson |first1=Mark |title=Celtic Champions League fixture dates revealed - details of Real Madrid, RB Leipzig and Shakhtar Donetsk ties |url=https://www.scotsman.com/sport/football/celtic/celtic-champions-league-fixture-dates-revealed-details-of-real-madrid-rb-leipzig-and-shakhtar-donetsk-ties-3821742 |website=scotsman.com |date=27 August 2022 |access-date=17 December 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Olley |first1=Declan |title=Real Madrid 5-1 Celtic: Champions League holders thrash Scottish champions to qualify for last 16 as group winners |url=https://www.skysports.com/football/news/11787/12735022/real-madrid-5-1-celtic-champions-league-holders-thrash-scottish-champions-to-qualify-for-last-16-as-group-winners |website=skysports.com |access-date=17 December 2025}}</ref>

In the 2023/24 season, Celtic played in the [[2023–24_UEFA_Champions_League_group_stage|group stage]] of the Champions League once again, being placed in a group with [[Atlético Madrid]], [[SS Lazio|Lazio]] and [[Feyenoord]], finishing bottom of the group with four points but did earn their first Champions League home victory in more than ten years with a 2-1 win over Feyenoord on match day six.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Media |first1=P. A. |title=Lagerbielke downs Feyenoord to end Celtic's dismal Champions League run |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2023/dec/13/lagerbielke-downs-feyenoord-to-end-celtics-dismal-champions-league-run |website=theguardian.com |date=13 December 2023 }}</ref>

In the 2024/25 season, Celtic played in the newly reformatted [[2024–25 UEFA Champions League league phase|league phase]] of the Champions League, ultimately finishing 21st in the table with three wins, three draws and two defeats, earning a place in the [[2024–25_UEFA_Champions_League_knockout_phase#Knockout_phase_play-offs|Knockout Phase Play-Offs]].<ref>{{cite web |title=League phase table |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/history/seasons/2025/groups/ |website=uefa.com |access-date=17 December 2025}}</ref> Celtic lost 3-2 on aggregate to [[Bayern Munich]], losing the first leg at [[Celtic Park]] 2-1 and conceding a late equaliser in Germany to draw 1-1.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Jones |first1=Rhys |title=Bayern Munich 'got away with one' to sneak into last 16 after face of monumental Celtic effort |url=https://www.tntsports.co.uk/football/champions-league/2024-2025/bayern-munich-got-away-with-one-last-16-celtic_sto20082605/story.shtml |website=tntsports.co.uk |access-date=17 December 2025}}</ref>

In the 2025/26 season, Celtic failed to qualify for the [[2025–26_UEFA_Champions_League_league_phase|league phase]] of the Champions League, losing to [[FC Kairat|Kairat Almaty]] 3-2 on penalties after both legs of the [[2025–26_UEFA_Champions_League_qualifying#Play-off_round|Play-off round]] tie failed to produce any goals, causing Celtic to drop into the [[2025–26_UEFA_Europa_League_league_phase|league phase]] of the [[2025–26_UEFA_Europa_League|Europa League]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Kazakh side Kairat Almaty dump Celtic from UCL playoff |url=https://www.espn.co.uk/football/report/_/gameId/756221 |website=espn.co.uk |access-date=17 December 2025}}</ref>

==Records==
{{Main|List of Celtic F.C. records and statistics}}
* Most appearances in European competition: [[Scott Brown (footballer, born June 1985)|Scott Brown]], 127<ref>{{cite news|title=Celtic 'full of character' – Rodgers |url=https://anshora.com/sports/celtic-full-of-character-rodgers/ |access-date=18 August 2016 |work=Anshora |date=18 August 2016 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160827160259/https://anshora.com/sports/celtic-full-of-character-rodgers/ |archive-date=27 August 2016}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=McNeill relives Celtic's Lisbon triumph|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0253-0d040a8d2d92-967b4b5539be-1000--mcneill-relives-celtic-s-lisbon-triumph/|website=UEFA|access-date=26 November 2015|date=17 September 2013}}</ref><ref name="sbfb">{{cite web|title=Brown, Scott|url=http://www.fitbastats.com/celtic/player.php?playerid=2934|website=FitbaStats|access-date=17 August 2021}}</ref>
* Most goals in European competition: [[Henrik Larsson]], 35<ref name="hl35" />
* First European match: [[Valencia CF|Valencia]] 4–2 Celtic, Inter-Cities Fairs Cup, first round, 24 October 1962<ref name="jimc" />
* First goal scored in Europe: [[Bobby Carroll]], against Valencia<ref name="cie" />
* Youngest first-team player in European competition: [[Karamoko Dembélé]], 16 years, 294 days (against [[CFR Cluj]], 12 December 2019)<ref>{{cite news |last=Oates |first=Kathleen |title=Karamoko Dembele enters history books as Celtic youngster makes European debut against CFR Clu |url=https://www.scotsman.com/sport/football/celtic/karamoko-dembele-enters-history-books-as-celtic-youngster-makes-european-debut-against-cfr-cluj-1-5061694 |access-date=17 December 2019 |work=The Scotsman |date=12 December 2019}}</ref>
* Biggest win: Celtic 9–0 [[Kokkolan Palloveikot|KPV Kokkola]], in the European Cup, 16 September 1970<ref>{{cite book|last=McGuirk|first=Brian|title=Celtic FC - the Ireland Connection|date=April 2009|publisher=Black and White Publishing|isbn=978-1-84502-248-8|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=Ah0jAwAAQBAJ&q=9-0+kpv+kokkola+%28Finland%29%2C+1970.e&pg=PT155|access-date=28 September 2015}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=Celtic F.C. 9 KPV Kokkola 0|url=http://www.celticprogrammesonline.com/PROGRAMME%20COVERS/7071/kpv/kokkola7071h.htm|website=Celtic Programmes Online|access-date=3 September 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140825094654/http://www.celticprogrammesonline.com/PROGRAMME%20COVERS/7071/kpv/kokkola7071h.htm |archive-date=25 August 2014}}</ref>
* Biggest defeat: [[FC Barcelona|Barcelona]] 7–0 Celtic in the UEFA Champions League, 13 September 2016<ref name="gubbed70" />
* Highest European home attendance (Hampden Park): 136,505, against [[Leeds United F.C.|Leeds United]] in the 1969–70 European Cup<ref name="ec6970" /><ref>{{cite web|last=Bloomfield|first=Craig|title=Top 10 biggest attendances in European Cup (Champions League) history – unbelievably huge crowds|url=https://talksport.com/football/130174/top-10-biggest-attendances-european-cup-champions-league-history-unbelievably-huge-crowds/|website=Talk Sport|access-date=12 April 2020|date=13 September 2013}}</ref>{{refn|group=fn|The home leg of Celtic's 1970 [[UEFA Champions League|European Cup]] semi-final tie against [[Leeds United F.C.|Leeds United]] was switched from [[Celtic Park]] (which had a capacity at the time of around 80,000) to [[Hampden Park]] due to the expected high demand for tickets.}}
::::::::::: (Celtic Park): 77,240, against [[ACF Fiorentina|Fiorentina]] in the 1969–70 European Cup<ref>{{cite web|title=3-0 Vs Fiorentina in European Champions Cup (04/03/1970)|url=http://www.fitbastats.com/celtic/game.php?gameid=76197|website=FitbaStats|access-date=23 November 2016}}</ref>
